/* Lecture 3 video lecture data: chapters, slide start times, timed bilingual transcript (generated). */
window.LEC = {"num":3,"videoBase":"https://wabyking.github.io/nlp2026-videos-1/lecture03/","parts":[{"n":1,"title":"Language Models in a Nutshell, and Why They Matter","blurb":"What a language model is, how a neural LM generates text word by word, and why next-word prediction on unlabeled text teaches so much.","file":"lecture03_part1.mp4","poster":"lecture03_part1.jpg","duration":646.6,"first":1,"last":20,"slides":[{"n":1,"title":"Title: Lecture 3","t":0.8},{"n":5,"title":"Today’s Lecture","t":34.67},{"n":6,"title":"What Is Language Modeling?","t":66.94},{"n":7,"title":"What Is Language Modeling?","t":94.4},{"n":8,"title":"What Is Language Modeling?","t":132.91},{"n":9,"title":"What Is Language Modeling?","t":174.03},{"n":10,"title":"Language Model Using Neural Networks","t":205.18},{"n":11,"title":"Autoregressive Generation with a Neural LM: 我思故我在","t":251.61},{"n":12,"title":"Background","t":355.95},{"n":13,"title":"Language Models: Narrow Sense","t":401.44},{"n":14,"title":"Language Models: Broad Sense","t":450.17},{"n":15,"title":"Today’s Lecture","t":484.46},{"n":16,"title":"LM (next word prediction) is scalable LM does not need annotations (data is scalable) Large LM generalizes to some exten","t":495.18},{"n":17,"title":"What Can We Learn from Reconstructing the Input?","t":552.39},{"n":18,"title":"What Can We Learn from Reconstructing the Input?","t":580.9},{"n":19,"title":"What Can We Learn from Reconstructing the Input?","t":602.71},{"n":20,"title":"What Can We Learn from Reconstructing the Input?","t":618.79}],"sents":[[0.8,3.96,1,"Good morning, everyone, and welcome back!","大家早上好，欢迎回来！"],[4.31,10.23,1,"This is Lecture three of our NLP course, and today's topic is language models.","这是我们 NLP 课程的第三讲，今天的主题是语言模型。"],[10.58,15.46,1,"Language models are probably the single most important idea in modern NLP.","语言模型可能是现代 NLP 中最重要的一个思想。"],[15.81,23.24,1,"Everything you hear about today, ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, DeepSeek, is, at its core, a language model.","如今你听到的一切，ChatGPT、Claude、Gemini、DeepSeek，本质上都是语言模型。"],[23.59,26.71,1,"So today we will build the idea from the ground up.","所以今天我们要从零开始把这个思想搭建起来。"],[27.06,33.27,1,"We go from simple counting-based n-gram models, all the way to BERT, T5 and GPT.","从简单的基于计数的 n-gram 模型，一直讲到 BERT、T5 和 GPT。"],[34.52,37.81,5,"Here is the plan for today. We have four parts.","这是今天的安排，一共四个部分。"],[38.16,42.79,5,"First, language models in a nutshell, together with a short recap.","第一，语言模型概览，外加一个简短的回顾。"],[43.14,48.26,5,"Second, why language models? Why is this simple task so powerful?","第二，为什么是语言模型？这个简单的任务为什么如此强大？"],[48.61,54.82,5,"Third, language models in the narrow sense: probability theory and n-gram models.","第三，狭义的语言模型：概率论和 n-gram 模型。"],[55.17,63.41,5,"And fourth, language models in the broad sense: word vectors, ELMo, BERT, T5, GPT and beyond.","第四，广义的语言模型：词向量、ELMo、BERT、T5、GPT 以及更多。"],[63.76,65.54,5,"Let's start with the first part.","我们从第一部分开始。"],[66.79,68.86,6,"So, what is language modeling?","那么，什么是语言建模？"],[69.21,72.49,6,"Before going further, think about it for a moment.","在继续之前，先花一点时间想一想。"],[72.84,76.78,6,"If I say a model of language, what do you think it should be able to do?","如果我说“一个语言的模型”，你觉得它应该能做什么？"],[77.13,84.6,6,"Here is the first answer: a language model assigns a probability to an N-gram, a sequence of n words.","第一个答案是：语言模型给一个 N-gram，也就是 n 个词组成的序列，赋予一个概率。"],[84.95,93.0,6,"Formally, it is a function f that maps a sequence of n words from the vocabulary V to a positive real number.","形式上，它是一个函数 f，把由词表 V 中 n 个词组成的序列映射到一个正实数。"],[94.25,95.63,7,"Here is the intuition.","我们来看直觉。"],[95.98,99.43,7,"Imagine a monkey typing randomly on a typewriter.","想象一只猴子在打字机上随机乱敲。"],[99.78,104.09,7,"It produces a random string of letters, something like this.","它会打出一串随机的字母，大概像这样。"],[104.44,110.53,7,"This is a valid string of characters, but it is very unlikely to be real language.","这是一串合法的字符，但它几乎不可能是真正的语言。"],[110.88,114.54,7,"So a language model should give it a low probability.","所以语言模型应该给它一个很低的概率。"],[114.89,118.5,7,"Now look at the sentence: ChatGPT is all you need.","再看这句话：ChatGPT is all you need。"],[118.85,124.9,7,"This looks like something a human would actually write, so a good language model gives it a high probability.","这看起来像是人真的会写出来的话，所以一个好的语言模型会给它很高的概率。"],[125.25,131.51,7,"So a language model is simply a model that tells us how likely a piece of text is.","所以，语言模型就是一个告诉我们一段文本有多大可能出现的模型。"],[132.76,135.35,8,"And you use language models every day.","而且你每天都在使用语言模型。"],[135.7,138.41,8,"Think about the input method on your phone.","想想你手机上的输入法。"],[138.76,143.31,8,"When you type I've, the keyboard suggests been, got, or never.","当你输入 I've 时，键盘会推荐 been、got 或 never。"],[143.66,149.84,8,"How does it know? It has a model that predicts which word is most likely to come next.","它怎么知道的？它有一个模型，可以预测下一个最可能出现的词。"],[150.19,158.19,8,"The slide calls this a conditional language model: it assigns a probability to a word given some conditioning context.","幻灯片上把它叫作条件语言模型：在给定一些上下文条件时，给一个词赋予概率。"],[158.54,166.75,8,"The probability of word n given words one to n minus one is f of the whole sequence, divided by f of the prefix.","给定第 1 到第 n−1 个词时第 n 个词的概率，等于整个序列的 f 值除以前缀的 f 值。"],[167.1,172.63,8,"That is exactly language modeling: given the previous words, predict the next word.","这正是语言建模：给定前面的词，预测下一个词。"],[173.88,179.52,9,"So let me put the two views together. A language model can be seen in two equivalent ways.","我们把两种视角放在一起。语言模型可以用两种等价的方式来看。"],[179.87,183.93,9,"One: it assigns a probability to a whole sentence.","第一：它给整个句子赋予一个概率。"],[184.28,188.05,9,"Two: it predicts the next word given the previous words.","第二：它在给定前面词的情况下预测下一个词。"],[188.4,194.37,9,"These two are connected by the chain rule of probability, which we will see in a few minutes.","这两者通过概率的链式法则联系起来，我们几分钟后就会看到。"],[194.72,203.78,9,"And notice the bottom line: this conditional probability of the next word is the foundation of modern large language models, like GPT and ChatGPT.","再注意最下面这一行：下一个词的条件概率，正是 GPT、ChatGPT 等现代大语言模型的基础。"],[205.03,209.74,10,"Today, language models are implemented with neural networks.","如今，语言模型是用神经网络来实现的。"],[210.09,219.26,10,"Look at this example. The input is a famous Chinese phrase, wo si gu wo, and the model has to predict the next character.","看这个例子。输入是一句有名的中文“我思故我”，模型要预测下一个字。"],[219.61,225.42,10,"The correct answer is zai, which completes wo si gu wo zai: I think, therefore I am.","正确答案是“在”，组成“我思故我在”：I think, therefore I am。"],[225.77,230.84,10,"Inside, the model is basically a black box: a large neural network.","内部来看，模型基本上是一个黑盒：一个庞大的神经网络。"],[231.19,239.24,10,"Large models like GPT-3 have one hundred seventy-five billion parameters, and today's frontier models are even bigger.","像 GPT-3 这样的大模型有 1750 亿个参数，而今天最前沿的模型还要更大。"],[239.59,244.87,10,"As a rough comparison, the human brain has around eighty-six billion neurons.","粗略对比一下，人脑大约有 860 亿个神经元。"],[245.22,250.21,10,"Of course, a parameter is not a neuron, but it gives you a sense of scale.","当然，参数不等于神经元，但这能让你对规模有个感觉。"],[251.46,259.71,11,"Let me open the black box a little, and show you how a neural language model actually generates this phrase, one character at a time.","我们把黑盒稍微打开一点，看看神经语言模型是怎样一个字一个字地生成这句话的。"],[260.06,262.46,11,"This is called autoregressive generation.","这叫作自回归生成。"],[262.81,268.96,11,"Each column is one position in the sequence, and each rounded box is a layer of the network at that position.","每一列是序列中的一个位置，每个圆角方框是该位置上网络的一层。"],[269.31,272.01,11,"The grey lines show which information can flow.","灰色的线表示信息可以怎样流动。"],[272.36,281.68,11,"A position can look at itself and at every position to its left, but never to the right. That is what causal means.","每个位置可以看到它自己和左边所有的位置，但永远看不到右边。这就是“因果”的含义。"],[282.03,284.78,11,"We start with only a special start token.","我们一开始只有一个特殊的起始符。"],[285.13,290.19,11,"The signal goes from the input into the first layer, then up through the layers.","信号从输入进入第一层，然后逐层向上传递。"],[290.54,297.91,11,"The top of the last position predicts the next character, wo, and that character is copied back down as the next input.","最后一个位置的顶层预测出下一个字“我”，然后这个字被复制回去作为下一个输入。"],[298.26,300.12,11,"Now we have two input tokens.","现在我们有两个输入词元。"],[300.47,308.42,11,"The new column receives information from both columns below it, so the new position combines information from all earlier tokens.","新的一列同时接收下面两列的信息，所以新位置融合了之前所有词元的信息。"],[308.77,310.79,11,"The model predicts si.","模型预测出“思”。"],[311.14,316.37,11,"The third step gives gu, the fourth step gives wo again, and the last step gives zai.","第三步得到“故”，第四步又得到“我”，最后一步得到“在”。"],[316.72,320.73,11,"We have generated wo si gu wo zai: I think, therefore I am.","我们生成了“我思故我在”：I think, therefore I am。"],[321.08,322.3,11,"Two things to remember.","有两点要记住。"],[322.65,328.46,11,"First, generation is a loop: predict one token, append it, and run again.","第一，生成是一个循环：预测一个词元，把它接上，再运行一次。"],[328.81,335.02,11,"Even GPT-4 or DeepSeek generates text exactly this way, one token at a time.","即使是 GPT-4 或 DeepSeek，也是完全这样一次一个词元地生成文本。"],[335.37,342.36,11,"Second, the earlier columns stay lit, because their computations do not change when we add new tokens on the right.","第二，前面的列一直保持点亮，因为在右边添加新词元时，它们的计算结果不会改变。"],[342.71,350.05,11,"So the model stores their keys and values and reuses them. That is the KV cache, which makes generation fast.","所以模型把它们的 key 和 value 存起来重复使用。这就是 KV 缓存，它让生成变得很快。"],[350.4,354.55,11,"We will come back to it when we compare model architectures later today.","今天稍后比较模型架构时，我们还会回到这一点。"],[355.8,362.62,12,"Here is a short history of language models and representations, from the book by Liu and colleagues.","这是语言模型与表示学习的简史，来自 Liu 等人的书。"],[362.97,366.05,12,"The big picture is a move from counting to prediction.","总体脉络是从“计数”走向“预测”。"],[366.4,369.89,12,"On this timeline, it starts with the n-gram model in nineteen forty-eight.","在这条时间线上，起点是 1948 年的 n-gram 模型。"],[370.24,375.23,12,"Then come the distributional hypothesis in nineteen fifty-four, and bag-of-words.","接着是 1954 年的分布假说，以及词袋模型。"],[375.58,380.0,12,"In nineteen eighty-six, we got distributed representations.","1986 年出现了分布式表示。"],[380.35,385.29,12,"In two thousand three, the neural probabilistic language model by Bengio.","2003 年，Bengio 提出了神经概率语言模型。"],[385.64,389.04,12,"In two thousand thirteen, word2vec.","2013 年，word2vec。"],[389.39,396.2,12,"And from two thousand eighteen, pre-trained language models like ELMo, BERT and GPT.","从 2018 年开始，出现了 ELMo、BERT 和 GPT 等预训练语言模型。"],[396.55,400.04,12,"Today's lecture follows this timeline quite closely.","今天的课基本就沿着这条时间线展开。"],[401.29,404.16,13,"Let's define language models in the narrow sense.","我们来定义狭义的语言模型。"],[404.51,414.44,13,"A language model is a probabilistic model that assigns a probability to every finite sequence of words, whether it is grammatical or not.","语言模型是一个概率模型，它给每一个有限长的词序列赋予概率，不管它是否合乎语法。"],[414.79,417.84,13,"Take the sentence: the cat sat on the mat.","以这句话为例：the cat sat on the mat。"],[418.19,427.34,13,"Using the chain rule, its probability is P of the, times P of cat given the, times P of sat given the cat, and so on.","用链式法则，它的概率是 P(the) 乘以 P(cat | the)，再乘以 P(sat | the cat)，依此类推。"],[427.69,434.64,13,"Notice there is an implicit left-to-right order: each word is conditioned on the words before it.","注意这里隐含了从左到右的顺序：每个词都以它前面的词为条件。"],[434.99,439.31,13,"And here is the key message: GPT-3 still works exactly this way.","关键信息是：GPT-3 仍然完全是这样工作的。"],[439.66,448.77,13,"The only difference is that each conditional probability is computed by a huge neural network with one hundred seventy-five billion parameters.","唯一的区别是，每个条件概率都由一个拥有 1750 亿参数的巨大神经网络来计算。"],[450.02,455.49,14,"In the broad sense, the term language model covers more architectures. There are three families.","在广义上，“语言模型”这个词涵盖更多架构，共有三大家族。"],[455.84,459.11,14,"Decoder-only models, like the GPT series.","仅解码器模型，比如 GPT 系列。"],[459.46,463.46,14,"Encoder-only models, like BERT, RoBERTa and ELECTRA.","仅编码器模型，比如 BERT、RoBERTa 和 ELECTRA。"],[463.81,467.72,14,"And encoder-decoder models, like T5 and BART.","以及编码器-解码器模型，比如 T5 和 BART。"],[468.07,472.26,14,"The last two families usually use a different pre-training objective.","后两类通常使用不同的预训练目标。"],[472.61,478.9,14,"For example, BERT uses masked language modeling instead of next-word prediction.","比如，BERT 用的是掩码语言建模，而不是下一个词预测。"],[479.25,483.06,14,"We will come back to each of them in the last part of the lecture.","在本讲的最后一部分，我们会逐一回到它们。"],[484.31,487.97,15,"Now let's move to the second part: why language models?","现在进入第二部分：为什么是语言模型？"],[488.32,493.78,15,"Why does such a simple task, predicting the next word, turn out to be so powerful?","为什么预测下一个词这样一个简单的任务，会变得如此强大？"],[495.03,496.71,16,"Here are several reasons.","这里有几个原因。"],[497.06,499.79,16,"First, language modeling is scalable.","第一，语言建模是可扩展的。"],[500.14,506.85,16,"It needs no human annotation; the text itself is the label. So the data can scale to trillions of words.","它不需要人工标注，文本本身就是标签。所以数据可以扩展到数万亿个词。"],[507.2,515.58,16,"Second, large language models generalize. They can transfer from task A to task B, for example through few-shot learning.","第二，大语言模型具有泛化能力，可以从任务 A 迁移到任务 B，比如通过少样本学习。"],[515.93,519.01,16,"Third, language is an efficient way to store knowledge.","第三，语言是一种高效存储知识的方式。"],[519.36,523.08,16,"Imagine trying to record all of Wikipedia as images instead of text.","想象一下，如果要用图片而不是文字来记录整个维基百科会怎样。"],[523.43,530.18,16,"Fourth, language is a format for thinking. Animals without rich language cannot reason that much.","第四，语言是一种思维的形式。没有丰富语言的动物，推理能力就很有限。"],[530.53,536.15,16,"Fifth, humans do language modeling every day; we constantly predict what comes next.","第五，人类每天都在做语言建模，我们时刻在预测接下来会发生什么。"],[536.5,539.87,16,"And there is naturally a lot of text available.","而且天然就有大量的文本可用。"],[540.22,546.73,16,"And finally, language is the communication interface between humans and computers, and even robots.","最后，语言是人与计算机、甚至与机器人之间的交流接口。"],[547.08,550.99,16,"We can describe actions and instructions in words.","我们可以用文字来描述动作和指令。"],[552.24,556.05,17,"Let's see what a model can learn just by predicting missing words.","我们来看看，仅仅通过预测缺失的词，模型能学到什么。"],[556.4,565.89,17,"Look at this sentence: I was thinking about the sequence that goes one, one, two, three, five, eight, thirteen, twenty-one, blank.","看这句话：我在想一个数列，1、1、2、3、5、8、13、21，空格。"],[566.24,568.69,17,"What's the answer? Yes, thirty-four.","答案是什么？对，34。"],[569.04,573.78,17,"To fill this blank, the model has to understand the Fibonacci sequence.","要填这个空，模型必须理解斐波那契数列。"],[574.13,579.5,17,"So by predicting the next word, the model is forced to learn some reasoning.","所以，通过预测下一个词，模型被迫学会一定的推理。"],[580.75,590.08,18,"Another example: Overall, the value I got from the two hours watching it was the sum total of the popcorn and the drink. The movie was blank.","另一个例子：总的来说，看这两个小时我得到的全部价值，就是爆米花和饮料。这部电影很空格。"],[590.43,592.85,18,"Boring or great? Clearly boring.","无聊还是精彩？显然是无聊。"],[593.2,596.64,18,"To predict this word, the model has to understand sentiment.","要预测这个词，模型必须理解情感。"],[596.99,601.31,18,"So language modeling teaches sentiment analysis for free.","所以语言建模免费教会了模型情感分析。"],[602.56,607.77,19,"Next one: The woman walked across the street, checking for traffic over blank shoulder.","下一个：那位女士过马路时，回头从空格肩膀上方查看来往车辆。"],[608.12,612.76,19,"Her or his? It should be her, because it refers back to the woman.","是 her 还是 his？应该是 her，因为它指代的是前面的“那位女士”。"],[613.11,617.39,19,"So the model has to learn coreference and context understanding.","所以模型必须学会指代消解和上下文理解。"],[618.64,624.59,20,"And one more: I went to the ocean to see the fish, turtles, seals, and blank.","再来一个：我去海边看鱼、海龟、海豹，还有空格。"],[624.94,627.22,20,"Possible answers are whales or dolphins.","可能的答案是鲸鱼或海豚。"],[627.57,633.31,20,"To get this right, the model needs commonsense knowledge about the world: what lives in the ocean.","要答对，模型需要关于世界的常识：海里生活着什么。"],[633.66,636.59,20,"So here is the lesson from these four examples.","所以，这四个例子给我们的启示是："],[636.94,644.79,20,"A simple objective, predicting missing words, forces the model to learn math, sentiment, syntax and world knowledge.","一个简单的目标，预测缺失的词，迫使模型学会数学、情感、句法和世界知识。"]]},{"n":2,"title":"Probability Foundations: Distributions and the Chain Rule","blurb":"A recap of the probability we need: sampling, joint and conditional probability, the chain rule and independence, leading to probability models of text.","file":"lecture03_part2.mp4","poster":"lecture03_part2.jpg","duration":360.6,"first":21,"last":33,"slides":[{"n":21,"title":"Today’s Lecture","t":0.8},{"n":22,"title":"Recap: Basic Probability Theory","t":11.57},{"n":23,"title":"Why Do We Need Language Models?","t":26.66},{"n":25,"title":"P( ) = 2/15","t":62.32},{"n":26,"title":"Sampling with Replacement","t":102.86},{"n":27,"title":"Sampling with Replacement","t":139.73},{"n":28,"title":"Probability Theory: Terminology","t":171.83},{"n":29,"title":"What Is a Probability Distribution?","t":210.09},{"n":30,"title":"Joint and Conditional Probability","t":238.29},{"n":31,"title":"The Chain Rule","t":261.59},{"n":32,"title":"Independence","t":307.38},{"n":33,"title":"Probability Models","t":326.22}],"sents":[[0.8,4.58,21,"Now we come to the third part: language models in the narrow sense.","现在进入第三部分：狭义的语言模型。"],[4.93,10.17,21,"We will first review some basic probability theory, and then build n-gram language models.","我们先复习一些基础概率论，然后搭建 n-gram 语言模型。"],[11.42,15.19,22,"Let's start with a quick recap of basic probability theory.","我们先快速回顾一下基础概率论。"],[15.54,19.38,22,"Many of you have seen this before, so I will go fast.","很多同学之前学过，所以我会讲得快一些。"],[19.73,25.26,22,"But please make sure you are comfortable with these ideas, because everything later builds on them.","但请务必熟悉这些概念，因为后面的一切都建立在它们之上。"],[26.51,30.64,23,"First, why do we need language models in NLP?","首先，NLP 中为什么需要语言模型？"],[30.99,34.77,23,"Many tasks need to produce natural language as output.","很多任务都需要输出自然语言。"],[35.12,41.3,23,"Machine translation returns text in the target language, and speech recognition returns a transcript.","机器翻译输出目标语言的文本，语音识别输出转写文本。"],[41.65,45.87,23,"Natural language generation returns text, and spell-checking returns corrected spelling.","自然语言生成输出文本，拼写检查输出纠正后的拼写。"],[46.22,50.14,23,"A language model defines a probability distribution over strings.","语言模型定义了字符串上的概率分布。"],[50.49,55.29,23,"So we can use it to score candidate outputs and choose the most likely one.","所以我们可以用它给候选输出打分，并选出最可能的那个。"],[55.64,60.92,23,"If P of A is larger than P of B, we return A, not B.","如果 P(A) 大于 P(B)，我们就返回 A，而不是 B。"],[62.17,63.53,25,"Let's use a simple game.","我们来玩一个简单的游戏。"],[63.88,70.81,25,"We have a bag of colored shapes: squares, circles and triangles in different colors, fifteen in total.","我们有一袋彩色形状：不同颜色的正方形、圆形和三角形，一共十五个。"],[71.16,75.99,25,"We pick a random shape and then put it back. This is sampling with replacement.","我们随机摸出一个形状，然后放回去。这叫有放回抽样。"],[76.34,82.22,25,"On the right, the same fifteen shapes are sorted by type and color, so they are easier to count.","右边是同样的十五个形状，按种类和颜色排好，更方便计数。"],[82.57,85.49,25,"From the counts we can compute probabilities.","根据计数，我们就能算出概率。"],[85.84,91.75,25,"For example, P of blue is five over fifteen, and P of red is also five over fifteen.","比如，P(蓝色) 是 5/15，P(红色) 也是 5/15。"],[92.1,95.16,25,"We can also compute conditional probabilities.","我们还可以计算条件概率。"],[95.51,101.46,25,"For example, the probability of a triangle given that it is red is three over five.","比如，在红色的条件下是三角形的概率是 3/5。"],[102.71,108.36,26,"Now, if we draw a sequence of shapes, what is the probability of a particular sequence?","那么，如果我们连续抽出一串形状，某个特定序列的概率是多少？"],[108.71,113.99,26,"Because we put each shape back, the draws are independent, so we just multiply.","因为每次都放回，各次抽取相互独立，所以直接相乘就行。"],[114.34,121.3,26,"For the first sequence, we get one fifteenth, times one fifteenth, times one fifteenth, times two fifteenths.","第一个序列是 1/15 乘 1/15 乘 1/15 再乘 2/15。"],[121.65,125.09,26,"That is two over fifty thousand six hundred twenty-five.","结果是 2/50625。"],[125.44,133.11,26,"The second sequence has probability thirty-six over fifty thousand six hundred twenty-five, so it is much more likely.","第二个序列的概率是 36/50625，所以它的可能性大得多。"],[133.46,138.33,26,"Keep this in mind: a sequence of shapes is just like a sequence of words.","记住这一点：一串形状就像一串词。"],[139.58,142.53,27,"In fact, we can do the same thing with text.","其实，我们可以对文本做同样的事情。"],[142.88,145.8,27,"Here is the opening of Alice in Wonderland.","这是《爱丽丝梦游仙境》的开头。"],[146.15,151.19,27,"Think of every word as a shape in a bag. There are sixty-six tokens here.","把每个词都看成袋子里的一个形状。这里一共有 66 个词元。"],[151.54,155.96,27,"Her appears twice, so P of her is two over sixty-six.","her 出现了两次，所以 P(her) 是 2/66。"],[156.31,159.71,27,"The comma appears four times, so four over sixty-six.","逗号出现了四次，所以是 4/66。"],[160.06,164.51,27,"Of appears three times, so three over sixty-six.","of 出现了三次，所以是 3/66。"],[164.86,170.43,27,"This is the simplest possible language model: just count how often each word appears.","这就是最简单的语言模型：只统计每个词出现的频率。"],[171.68,172.92,28,"Some terminology.","介绍一些术语。"],[173.27,179.1,28,"A trial, or experiment, is something like picking a shape or predicting a word.","试验，也叫实验，就是像摸一个形状或预测一个词这样的操作。"],[179.45,187.6,28,"The sample space, omega, is the set of all possible outcomes: all shapes, or all words in Alice in Wonderland.","样本空间 Ω 是所有可能结果的集合：所有形状，或《爱丽丝梦游仙境》中的所有词。"],[187.95,194.94,28,"An event is a subset of the sample space, like predicting the word the, or picking a triangle.","事件是样本空间的一个子集，比如预测出 the 这个词，或者摸到一个三角形。"],[195.29,199.5,28,"And a random variable is a function from the sample space to some set.","随机变量是从样本空间到某个集合的函数。"],[199.85,208.69,28,"It gives a measurement of interest. For example: did we pick Alice, did we pick a noun, or a word starting with x?","它给出我们关心的某种度量。比如：我们摸到的是 Alice 吗？是名词吗？是以 x 开头的词吗？"],[209.94,216.87,29,"What is a probability distribution? P defines a distribution over omega if three conditions hold.","什么是概率分布？如果满足三个条件，P 就定义了 Ω 上的一个分布。"],[217.22,220.55,29,"One, every event has a probability between zero and one.","一，每个事件的概率都在 0 到 1 之间。"],[220.9,224.26,29,"Two, the empty event has probability zero.","二，空事件的概率为 0。"],[224.61,231.37,29,"Three, the probabilities of disjoint events that together cover the whole space sum to one.","三，互不相交且合起来覆盖整个空间的事件，概率之和为 1。"],[231.72,236.89,29,"That's all. Later we must check that our language models actually satisfy these conditions.","就这些。后面我们要检查语言模型是否真的满足这些条件。"],[238.14,241.15,30,"Next, joint and conditional probability.","接下来是联合概率和条件概率。"],[241.5,249.03,30,"The conditional probability of X given Y is defined as the joint probability of X and Y, divided by the probability of Y.","给定 Y 时 X 的条件概率，定义为 X 和 Y 的联合概率除以 Y 的概率。"],[249.38,256.12,30,"Back to our bag: the probability that a shape is blue, given that it is a square, is two over five.","回到我们的袋子：已知是正方形时，它是蓝色的概率是 2/5。"],[256.47,260.19,30,"We only look inside the squares, and count the blue ones.","我们只看正方形里面，数其中蓝色的有几个。"],[261.44,268.81,31,"From this definition we get: the joint probability P of X and Y equals P of X given Y, times P of Y.","由这个定义可以得到：联合概率 P(X, Y) 等于 P(X | Y) 乘以 P(Y)。"],[269.16,273.55,31,"If we apply this repeatedly to many variables, we get the chain rule.","把它反复应用到多个变量上，就得到了链式法则。"],[273.9,284.93,31,"The probability of X one to X n equals P of X one, times P of X two given X one, times P of X three given X one and X two, and so on.","X1 到 Xn 的联合概率等于 P(X1)，乘以 P(X2 | X1)，再乘以 P(X3 | X1, X2)，依此类推。"],[285.28,294.99,31,"In compact form, it is P of X one, times the product over i from two to n of P of X i given all the previous variables.","写成紧凑形式，就是 P(X1) 乘以 i 从 2 到 n 的连乘，每一项是给定之前所有变量时 Xi 的概率。"],[295.34,298.01,31,"This is the most important formula of today.","这是今天最重要的公式。"],[298.36,305.98,31,"This is exactly how a language model scores a sentence: word by word, each conditioned on all previous words.","语言模型正是这样给句子打分的：逐词计算，每个词都以前面所有的词为条件。"],[307.23,316.14,32,"Independence. Two random variables X and Y are independent if P of X and Y equals P of X times P of Y.","独立性。如果 P(X, Y) 等于 P(X) 乘以 P(Y)，那么随机变量 X 和 Y 就是独立的。"],[316.49,321.23,32,"And if they are independent, then P of X given Y is just P of X.","如果它们独立，那么 P(X | Y) 就等于 P(X)。"],[321.58,324.82,32,"In other words, knowing Y tells you nothing about X.","换句话说，知道 Y 对了解 X 没有任何帮助。"],[326.07,329.25,33,"Building a probability model has two steps.","构建一个概率模型分两步。"],[329.6,336.38,33,"First, define the model. Second, estimate its parameters, which is what we call training or learning.","第一，定义模型。第二，估计它的参数，也就是我们说的训练或学习。"],[336.73,340.84,33,"And models almost always make independence assumptions.","而且模型几乎总会做独立性假设。"],[341.19,346.92,33,"Even if X and Y are not really independent, the model may treat them as if they were.","即使 X 和 Y 实际上并不独立，模型也可能把它们当作独立的来处理。"],[347.27,354.25,33,"Why? Because it reduces the number of parameters we need to estimate, for example from n squared to two n.","为什么？因为这能减少需要估计的参数数量，比如从 n 的平方降到 2n。"],[354.6,358.73,33,"This trade-off is exactly the idea behind n-gram models.","这种权衡正是 n-gram 模型背后的思想。"]]},{"n":3,"title":"N-gram Language Models: Counting and Estimation","blurb":"Unigram and bigram models for an Alice passage, maximum-likelihood estimation by counting, and the practical fixes of UNK and sentence-start tokens.","file":"lecture03_part3.mp4","poster":"lecture03_part3.jpg","duration":362.3,"first":34,"last":46,"slides":[{"n":34,"title":"Language Modeling with N-grams Counting-based instead of prediction-based in Neural Networks","t":0.8},{"n":35,"title":"Language Modeling with N-grams","t":15.85},{"n":36,"title":"N-gram Models","t":48.97},{"n":37,"title":"A Unigram Model for Alice","t":73.7},{"n":38,"title":"A Bigram Model for Alice","t":106.48},{"n":39,"title":"How to Estimate Probabilities in LM?","t":135.64},{"n":40,"title":"Learning (Estimating) a Language Model","t":144.66},{"n":41,"title":"Are N-gram Models Actual Language Models?","t":187.52},{"n":42,"title":"How Do N-gram Models Define P(L)?","t":202.09},{"n":43,"title":"How Do N-gram Models Define P(L)?","t":242.21},{"n":44,"title":"A Couple More Modifications…","t":285.51},{"n":45,"title":"Handling Unknown Words: UNK","t":291.23},{"n":46,"title":"What About the Beginning of the Sentence?","t":328.59}],"sents":[[0.8,7.48,34,"Now, with this probability background, let's build our first real language model: language modeling with n-grams.","有了这些概率基础，我们来搭建第一个真正的语言模型：基于 n-gram 的语言建模。"],[7.83,14.45,34,"Notice the subtitle: these models are counting-based, instead of prediction-based like neural networks.","注意副标题：这些模型是基于计数的，而不是像神经网络那样基于预测的。"],[15.7,24.63,35,"A language model over a vocabulary V assigns probabilities to strings drawn from V star, that is, all finite sequences of words.","词表 V 上的语言模型给 V 星中的字符串赋予概率，也就是所有有限长的词序列。"],[24.98,32.91,35,"Recall the chain rule: the probability of a sentence is the product of each word's probability given all previous words.","回忆链式法则：一个句子的概率，等于每个词在给定前面所有词时的概率之积。"],[33.26,40.6,35,"The problem is that conditioning on the full history is impossible to estimate; most long histories never appear in our data.","问题在于，以完整历史为条件根本无法估计，因为大多数长历史在数据中从未出现过。"],[40.95,47.57,35,"So an n-gram model makes an assumption: each word depends only on the last n minus one words.","所以 n-gram 模型做了一个假设：每个词只依赖于前面 n−1 个词。"],[48.82,50.75,36,"Here are the most common cases.","这里是最常见的几种情况。"],[51.1,57.33,36,"A unigram model: each word is independent, so we just multiply P of each word.","一元模型：每个词相互独立，所以直接把每个词的概率相乘。"],[57.68,61.59,36,"A bigram model: each word depends only on the previous word.","二元模型：每个词只依赖于前一个词。"],[61.94,66.26,36,"A trigram model: each word depends on the previous two words.","三元模型：每个词依赖于前两个词。"],[66.61,72.3,36,"These independence assumptions are called Markov assumptions, of order n minus one.","这些独立性假设被称为 n−1 阶马尔可夫假设。"],[73.55,77.5,37,"Let's look at a unigram model trained on our Alice text.","我们来看在爱丽丝文本上训练的一元模型。"],[77.85,87.64,37,"If we generate from it, we get something like: beginning by, very Alice but was and? reading no tired of to into sitting.","如果用它来生成，会得到类似这样的东西：beginning by, very Alice but was and? reading no tired of to into sitting。"],[87.99,89.44,37,"It's just word salad.","这就是一盘“词语沙拉”。"],[89.79,94.05,37,"Why? Because a unigram model ignores word order completely.","为什么？因为一元模型完全忽略了词序。"],[94.4,102.39,37,"In this model, the probability of a real English sentence is the same as the probability of the same words shuffled randomly.","在这个模型中，一个真实英文句子的概率，和把这些词随机打乱后的概率是一样的。"],[102.74,105.08,37,"That is clearly not good enough.","这显然不够好。"],[106.33,110.29,38,"Now a bigram model. Here we condition on the previous word.","再来看二元模型。这里我们以前一个词为条件。"],[110.64,120.05,38,"For example, after the word the, we saw bank, book and use, once each, so each gets probability one third.","比如，在 the 后面，我们看到了 bank、book 和 use，各一次，所以每个的概率是三分之一。"],[120.4,125.79,38,"After tired, we always saw of, so P of of given tired is one.","在 tired 后面，总是出现 of，所以 P(of | tired) 等于 1。"],[126.14,131.4,38,"After was, we saw beginning and reading, each with probability one half.","在 was 后面，出现了 beginning 和 reading，概率各为二分之一。"],[131.75,134.24,38,"This already captures some local word order.","这已经捕捉到了一些局部的词序。"],[135.49,137.79,39,"So far I have just shown you the numbers.","到目前为止，我只是把数字展示给你们看。"],[138.14,143.26,39,"But how exactly do we estimate these probabilities? Let's look at that now.","但这些概率究竟是怎么估计出来的？我们现在来看。"],[144.51,146.49,40,"Where do the parameters come from?","参数从哪里来？"],[146.84,152.86,40,"For example, what is the probability of the, given that the previous word is on?","比如，在前一个词是 on 的情况下，the 的概率是多少？"],[153.21,156.06,40,"We need a large amount of text as training data.","我们需要大量文本作为训练数据。"],[156.41,161.85,40,"The most basic technique is relative frequency estimation, which is just counting.","最基本的方法是相对频率估计，其实就是计数。"],[162.2,166.72,40,"Count how many times on the appears, and divide by how many times on appears.","统计 on the 出现的次数，再除以 on 出现的次数。"],[167.07,171.79,40,"This is also called Maximum Likelihood Estimation, or MLE.","这也叫最大似然估计，简称 MLE。"],[172.14,179.24,40,"One important note: MLE puts all probability mass on events that appear in the training corpus.","有一点很重要：MLE 把所有概率质量都分给了在训练语料中出现过的事件。"],[179.59,186.12,40,"Anything we have never seen gets probability zero. Remember this; it will be a problem soon.","任何没见过的东西概率都是零。记住这一点，它很快就会成为问题。"],[187.37,192.37,41,"Here is a question for you: are n-gram models actually language models?","问大家一个问题：n-gram 模型真的是语言模型吗？"],[192.72,198.86,41,"In other words, do they really define a proper probability distribution over all strings of a language?","换句话说，它们真的在一门语言的所有字符串上定义了一个合法的概率分布吗？"],[199.21,200.69,41,"Think about it for a second.","想一想。"],[201.94,208.2,42,"An n-gram model computes the probability of a sentence as a product of word predictions.","n-gram 模型把句子的概率计算为每个词预测概率的乘积。"],[208.55,217.71,42,"With a fixed vocabulary, it is easy to make sure that each conditional, P of word i given the previous word, is a proper distribution.","在固定词表下，很容易保证每个条件概率，即给定前一个词时第 i 个词的概率，是一个合法的分布。"],[218.06,222.07,42,"The values are between zero and one, and they sum to one.","取值在 0 到 1 之间，并且总和为 1。"],[222.42,226.87,42,"But then the model only defines one distribution for each sentence length N.","但这样一来，模型只是对每个句子长度 N 分别定义了一个分布。"],[227.22,233.34,42,"The strings of a real language don't all have the same length: yes, I agree, I see you.","真实语言中的字符串长度各不相同：yes、I agree、I see you。"],[233.69,235.36,42,"And there is no maximum length.","而且也没有最大长度。"],[235.71,240.81,42,"The solution is simple: add a special end-of-sentence token, EOS.","解决办法很简单：加一个特殊的句末标记 EOS。"],[242.06,245.77,43,"Think of a language model as a stochastic process.","把语言模型看作一个随机过程。"],[246.12,253.47,43,"At each time step, we randomly pick one more word. We stop when we pick the special end-of-sentence token.","每个时间步，我们随机再选一个词。当选到特殊的句末标记时就停止。"],[253.82,261.65,43,"To make this possible, we add EOS to the end of every training sentence, so our vocabulary becomes V plus EOS.","为此，我们在每个训练句子末尾加上 EOS，于是词表变成 V 加上 EOS。"],[262.0,267.38,43,"Now we get an actual language model: a single distribution over strings of any length.","现在我们得到了一个真正的语言模型：一个覆盖任意长度字符串的单一分布。"],[267.73,275.77,43,"Technically, this works because the probability of EOS is high enough that we always stop after a finite number of words.","严格来说，这之所以成立，是因为 EOS 的概率足够高，保证我们总会在有限个词之后停下来。"],[276.12,284.11,43,"Why do we care? Because now we can compare the probabilities of strings of different lengths, since they come from the same distribution.","为什么要在意这个？因为现在我们可以比较不同长度字符串的概率了，它们来自同一个分布。"],[285.36,289.83,44,"We need a couple more modifications to make n-gram models work in practice.","要让 n-gram 模型在实践中可用，还需要再做几处修改。"],[291.08,293.22,45,"The first is unknown words.","第一个是未登录词。"],[293.57,299.93,45,"During training, we fix a vocabulary, for example all words that appear at least n times.","训练时，我们先固定一个词表，比如所有至少出现 n 次的词。"],[300.28,307.56,45,"All other words are replaced by a special token, UNK, and we estimate the model on this modified corpus.","其他所有词都替换成一个特殊标记 UNK，然后在修改后的语料上估计模型。"],[307.91,313.85,45,"At test time, any word not in the vocabulary is also replaced by UNK.","测试时，任何不在词表中的词也都替换成 UNK。"],[314.2,321.47,45,"A refinement is to use different UNK tokens for different types of words, for example one for numbers.","一种改进是为不同类型的词使用不同的 UNK 标记，比如专门给数字一个。"],[321.82,327.19,45,"Later, in modern models, subword tokenization largely solves this problem.","后来在现代模型中，子词切分在很大程度上解决了这个问题。"],[328.44,332.18,46,"The second modification is about the beginning of a sentence.","第二个修改与句子开头有关。"],[332.53,340.63,46,"In a trigram model, the first word only has a unigram probability, and the second word only has a bigram probability.","在三元模型中，第一个词只有一元概率，第二个词只有二元概率。"],[340.98,343.71,46,"Only the third term is a real trigram.","只有第三项才是真正的三元概率。"],[344.06,351.01,46,"If this bothers you, add n minus one beginning-of-sentence symbols, BOS, to each sentence.","如果你介意这一点，可以在每个句子前加上 n−1 个句首符号 BOS。"],[351.36,355.64,46,"For example: BOS, BOS, Alice was, and so on.","例如：BOS、BOS、Alice was，等等。"],[355.99,360.4,46,"Now every word, even the first one, has a full trigram context.","这样每个词，即使是第一个词，也都有完整的三元上下文。"]]},{"n":4,"title":"Generating Text: Spinning the N-gram Wheel","blurb":"How to sample from a distribution, and step-by-step roulette-wheel generation with bigram and trigram models trained on the Alice passage.","file":"lecture03_part4.mp4","poster":"lecture03_part4.jpg","duration":414.8,"first":47,"last":60,"slides":[{"n":47,"title":"Using N-gram Models to Generate Language","t":0.8},{"n":48,"title":"Which language model generates more fluent text? A. Unigram B. Bigram C. Trigram D. N-gram with a larger N","t":7.08},{"n":49,"title":"Generating from a Distribution","t":35.76},{"n":50,"title":"Generating with a Bigram Model: Spin the Wheel (1/5)","t":72.04},{"n":51,"title":"Generating with a Bigram Model: Spin the Wheel (2/5)","t":130.87},{"n":52,"title":"Generating with a Bigram Model: Spin the Wheel (3/5)","t":163.54},{"n":53,"title":"Generating with a Bigram Model: Spin the Wheel (4/5)","t":192.28},{"n":54,"title":"Generating with a Bigram Model: Spin the Wheel (5/5)","t":209.33},{"n":55,"title":"Generating with a Trigram Model: Spin the Wheel (1/6)","t":261.37},{"n":56,"title":"Generating with a Trigram Model: Spin the Wheel (2/6)","t":299.39},{"n":57,"title":"Generating with a Trigram Model: Spin the Wheel (3/6)","t":311.33},{"n":58,"title":"Generating with a Trigram Model: Spin the Wheel (4/6)","t":339.39},{"n":59,"title":"Generating with a Trigram Model: Spin the Wheel (5/6)","t":348.52},{"n":60,"title":"Generating with a Trigram Model: Spin the Wheel (6/6)","t":355.48}],"sents":[[0.8,5.68,47,"Now let's see what happens when we use n-gram models to generate language.","现在我们来看看用 n-gram 模型生成语言会发生什么。"],[6.93,11.11,48,"A quick quiz. Which language model generates more fluent text?","来个小测验。哪种语言模型生成的文本更流畅？"],[11.46,20.84,48,"A, unigram. B, bigram. C, trigram. D, an n-gram with a larger N.","A，一元模型。B，二元模型。C，三元模型。D，N 更大的 n-gram 模型。"],[21.19,23.3,48,"Take a few seconds and think about it.","花几秒钟想一想。"],[23.65,30.55,48,"The answer is D: larger N usually gives more fluent text, because the model sees more context.","答案是 D：N 越大，生成的文本通常越流畅，因为模型看到了更多上下文。"],[30.9,34.36,48,"But as we'll see, there is a price to pay.","但我们会看到，这是要付出代价的。"],[35.61,38.76,49,"First, how do we generate text from a distribution?","首先，怎样从一个分布中生成文本？"],[39.11,44.23,49,"Suppose X has N possible outcomes, with probabilities p one to p N.","假设 X 有 N 个可能的取值，概率分别是 p1 到 pN。"],[44.58,52.81,49,"We divide the interval from zero to one into N small pieces, one for each outcome, with lengths p one, p two, and so on.","我们把 0 到 1 的区间分成 N 小段，每个取值一段，长度分别是 p1、p2，依此类推。"],[53.16,59.33,49,"Then we draw a random number r between zero and one, and return the outcome whose interval contains r.","然后在 0 到 1 之间抽一个随机数 r，返回 r 所落入区间对应的取值。"],[59.68,64.35,49,"It's like spinning a roulette wheel where each slot has a different size.","这就像转一个轮盘，只不过每个格子大小不同。"],[64.7,70.64,49,"To generate a sentence, we just repeat this, word by word, until we pick EOS.","要生成一个句子，就逐词重复这个过程，直到选中 EOS 为止。"],[71.89,74.39,50,"Let's make this concrete with a little game.","我们用一个小游戏把它具体化。"],[74.74,83.16,50,"We take the Alice passage from before and build a bigram model from it. To generate text, we just spin a roulette wheel again and again.","我们拿前面那段爱丽丝的文本建立一个二元模型。要生成文本，就一次又一次地转轮盘。"],[83.51,85.27,50,"We start from the word the.","我们从 the 这个词开始。"],[85.62,91.46,50,"In our passage, the is followed by bank once, book once and use once.","在这段文本中，the 后面分别跟了一次 bank、一次 book 和一次 use。"],[91.81,101.54,50,"So P of bank, book and use given the is one third each. That is exactly how the wheel is divided: three equal slices.","所以给定 the 时，bank、book、use 的概率各为三分之一。轮盘正是这样分成三等份的。"],[101.89,108.84,50,"The bar on the right is the same distribution drawn on the interval from zero to one, just like on the previous slide.","右边的条形图是同一个分布，画在 0 到 1 的区间上，和上一页一样。"],[109.19,114.08,50,"Spinning the wheel is the same as drawing a random number r between zero and one.","转一次轮盘，就相当于在 0 到 1 之间抽一个随机数 r。"],[114.43,117.25,50,"Let's spin. The wheel stops at book.","我们来转一下。轮盘停在了 book 上。"],[117.6,127.16,50,"Equivalently, r is zero point five two, which falls into the interval of book, from zero point three three to zero point six seven.","等价地说，r 是 0.52，落在 book 的区间里，也就是 0.33 到 0.67 之间。"],[127.51,129.47,50,"So our next word is book.","所以下一个词是 book。"],[130.72,132.48,51,"Now the previous word is book.","现在前一个词是 book。"],[132.83,139.96,51,"In the passage, book is followed once by her, in the book her sister, and once by a comma, in a book, thought Alice.","文本中，book 后面一次跟的是 her，出自 the book her sister；另一次跟的是逗号，出自 a book, thought Alice。"],[140.31,147.12,51,"So the wheel now has only two halves: her and the comma, each with probability one half.","所以轮盘现在只有两半：her 和逗号，概率各为二分之一。"],[147.47,155.67,51,"Notice that the wheel changes at every step, because it depends on the previous word. That is the Markov assumption in action.","注意，每一步的轮盘都不一样，因为它取决于前一个词。这就是马尔可夫假设在起作用。"],[156.02,162.14,51,"Spin. r is zero point three one, which is in the first half, so the next word is her.","转一下。r 是 0.31，落在前一半，所以下一个词是 her。"],[163.39,164.99,52,"The previous word is her.","前一个词是 her。"],[165.34,173.01,52,"Both times her appears, it is followed by sister. So the wheel has only one slice: P of sister given her is one.","her 出现的两次后面都跟着 sister。所以轮盘只有一块：P(sister | her) 等于 1。"],[173.36,175.92,52,"No matter where the wheel stops, we get sister.","无论轮盘停在哪里，得到的都是 sister。"],[176.27,180.32,52,"r is zero point seven four, and the next word is sister.","r 是 0.74，下一个词是 sister。"],[180.67,184.62,52,"This shows a property of n-gram models trained on small data.","这体现了在小数据上训练的 n-gram 模型的一个特点。"],[184.97,190.88,52,"Many contexts have only one continuation, so the model simply copies the training text.","很多上下文只有一种后续，所以模型只是在照抄训练文本。"],[192.13,193.8,53,"The previous word is sister.","前一个词是 sister。"],[194.15,200.9,53,"We saw sister on the bank, and sister was reading. So on and was each get probability one half.","我们见过 sister on the bank 和 sister was reading。所以 on 和 was 的概率各为二分之一。"],[201.25,207.93,53,"Spin. r is zero point six eight, in the second half, so the next word is was.","转一下。r 是 0.68，落在后一半，所以下一个词是 was。"],[209.18,214.7,54,"Last step. The previous word is was: was beginning and was reading, one half each.","最后一步。前一个词是 was：was beginning 和 was reading，各占一半。"],[215.05,220.14,54,"Spin. r is zero point eight seven, so the next word is reading.","转一下。r 是 0.87，所以下一个词是 reading。"],[220.49,223.88,54,"And we have generated: the book her sister was reading.","我们生成了：the book her sister was reading。"],[224.23,229.28,54,"Every word was one spin of a different wheel, P of w given the previous word.","每个词都是转了一次不同的轮盘，也就是给定前一个词时 w 的概率。"],[229.63,230.75,54,"Two observations.","有两点观察。"],[231.1,238.08,54,"First, the sentence looks fluent locally, because each pair of words appeared in the training text.","第一，这个句子在局部看起来很流畅，因为每一对相邻的词都在训练文本中出现过。"],[238.43,246.8,54,"Second, if we spin again, we can get a different sentence, for example: the bank, and of having nothing to do.","第二，如果再转一次，我们可能得到不同的句子，比如：the bank, and of having nothing to do。"],[247.15,253.92,54,"With more data and larger n, the wheels get more slices, and the text gets more varied and more fluent.","数据更多、n 更大时，轮盘的格子会更多，文本也会更多样、更流畅。"],[254.27,259.97,54,"We will see exactly that with the Wall Street Journal and Shakespeare examples soon.","我们很快会在华尔街日报和莎士比亚的例子中看到这一点。"],[261.22,264.55,55,"Now let's play the same game with a trigram model.","现在我们用三元模型玩同样的游戏。"],[264.9,269.42,55,"The only change: each wheel now depends on the previous two words instead of one.","唯一的变化是：每个轮盘现在取决于前两个词，而不是一个。"],[269.77,273.21,55,"We start from the same context as before: the book.","我们从和之前一样的上下文开始：the book。"],[273.56,280.82,55,"How often is the book followed by each word? In the passage, the book appears only once, followed by her.","the book 后面各个词出现了几次？在文本中，the book 只出现了一次，后面跟着 her。"],[281.17,286.11,55,"So the wheel has a single slice: P of her given the book is one.","所以轮盘只有一块：P(her | the book) 等于 1。"],[286.46,295.39,55,"Remember that with the bigram model, the first wheel after the had three slices. With two words of context, the choice is already gone.","还记得在二元模型中，the 之后的第一个轮盘有三块吗？有了两个词的上下文，选择已经没有了。"],[295.74,297.99,55,"Of course, the wheel lands on her.","当然，轮盘停在了 her 上。"],[299.24,301.12,56,"The context is now book her.","现在的上下文是 book her。"],[301.47,307.95,56,"Again it appears only once, followed by sister. One slice, probability one.","它同样只出现了一次，后面跟着 sister。只有一块，概率为 1。"],[308.3,309.93,56,"The next word is sister.","下一个词是 sister。"],[311.18,313.33,57,"The context is her sister.","上下文是 her sister。"],[313.68,316.67,57,"This is the only interesting wheel in the whole example.","这是整个例子中唯一有意思的轮盘。"],[317.02,325.88,57,"Her sister appears twice: once followed by on, in her sister on the bank, and once by was, in her sister was reading.","her sister 出现了两次：一次后面是 on，出自 her sister on the bank；一次是 was，出自 her sister was reading。"],[326.23,327.85,57,"So we get two halves.","所以轮盘分成两半。"],[328.2,333.37,57,"r is zero point two nine, in the first half, so this time the next word is on.","r 是 0.29，落在前一半，所以这次下一个词是 on。"],[333.72,337.99,57,"Notice that the bigram run went the other way and picked was.","注意，二元模型那一轮走了另一条路，选的是 was。"],[339.24,345.25,58,"The context is sister on, which appears once, followed by the. One slice again.","上下文是 sister on，只出现过一次，后面跟着 the。又是只有一块。"],[345.6,347.12,58,"The next word is the.","下一个词是 the。"],[348.37,352.18,59,"The context is on the, followed only by bank.","上下文是 on the，后面只跟过 bank。"],[352.53,354.08,59,"The next word is bank.","下一个词是 bank。"],[355.33,359.29,60,"One more word to finish the phrase. The context is now the bank.","再来一个词把这个短语补完。现在的上下文是 the bank。"],[359.64,365.17,60,"In the passage, it is followed by a comma, in on the bank, and of having nothing to do.","在文本中，它后面跟的是逗号，出自 on the bank, and of having nothing to do。"],[365.52,373.19,60,"Remember that our model treats punctuation as tokens, just like words. So the wheel has one slice: the comma.","记住，我们的模型把标点也当作词元，和单词一样。所以轮盘只有一块：逗号。"],[373.54,378.73,60,"We get: the book her sister on the bank, followed by a comma.","我们得到：the book her sister on the bank，后面跟一个逗号。"],[379.08,384.08,60,"The model has generated a complete phrase, and is ready to continue with and.","模型生成了一个完整的短语，接下来准备接上 and。"],[384.43,385.35,60,"Two lessons.","两点启示。"],[385.7,392.94,60,"First, this is a new sentence that never appears in the passage, because we stitched two pieces of the text together at her sister.","第一，这是一个原文中从未出现过的新句子，因为我们在 her sister 处把文本的两个片段拼接到了一起。"],[393.29,397.15,60,"Second, five of the six wheels had a single slice.","第二，六个轮盘中有五个只有一块。"],[397.5,404.91,60,"With a larger n, each context is seen fewer times, so the counts become sparse and the model mostly copies the training data.","n 越大，每个上下文出现的次数越少，计数变得稀疏，模型基本上就是在照抄训练数据。"],[405.26,412.94,60,"That is exactly the sparsity problem we will discuss, and the reason we need much more data, smoothing, or neural language models.","这正是我们接下来要讨论的稀疏性问题，也是我们需要更多数据、平滑方法或神经语言模型的原因。"]]},{"n":5,"title":"Sparsity, Smoothing and Evaluation: Perplexity and WER","blurb":"Why bigger n gives more fluent but less diverse text, how add-one smoothing handles unseen events, and how perplexity and word error rate evaluate LMs.","file":"lecture03_part5.mp4","poster":"lecture03_part5.jpg","duration":641.5,"first":61,"last":82,"slides":[{"n":61,"title":"Generating the Wall Street Journal","t":0.8},{"n":62,"title":"Generating Shakespeare","t":28.09},{"n":63,"title":"What is the drawback of a large N for an N-gram LM? Space complexity Less diversity?","t":49.06},{"n":64,"title":"Shakespeare as Corpus","t":75.95},{"n":65,"title":"MLE Doesn’t Capture Unseen Events","t":118.16},{"n":66,"title":"How Do We Assign Non-zero Probability to Unseen Events?","t":157.69},{"n":67,"title":"Smoothing Methods","t":188.82},{"n":68,"title":"Add-One (Laplace) Smoothing","t":224.71},{"n":69,"title":"Evaluation","t":259.09},{"n":70,"title":"Intrinsic vs Extrinsic Evaluation","t":267.16},{"n":71,"title":"Intrinsic Evaluation of Language Models: Perplexity","t":298.43},{"n":72,"title":"Perplexity","t":308.03},{"n":73,"title":"Practical Issues","t":355.71},{"n":74,"title":"Extrinsic (Task-Based) Evaluation of LMs: Word Error Rate","t":382.68},{"n":75,"title":"Intrinsic vs. Extrinsic Evaluation","t":393.8},{"n":76,"title":"Language Models for Downstream Tasks","t":421.96},{"n":77,"title":"An Example of Using Language Models in ASR","t":463.07},{"n":78,"title":"Word Error Rate (WER)","t":492.03},{"n":79,"title":"Which One is Better ? Using Language Models in MT","t":531.54},{"n":80,"title":"Let the Language Model Decide","t":568.56},{"n":81,"title":"To Recap….","t":602.05},{"n":82,"title":"Key Concepts in Summary","t":607.65}],"sents":[[0.8,4.33,61,"Here are samples from models trained on the Wall Street Journal.","这些是在华尔街日报语料上训练的模型生成的样例。"],[4.68,7.11,61,"The unigram output is nonsense.","一元模型的输出毫无意义。"],[7.46,11.85,61,"The bigram output has some local structure, but no global meaning.","二元模型的输出有一些局部结构，但没有整体含义。"],[12.2,19.82,61,"The trigram output says: They also point to ninety nine point six billion dollars from two hundred four oh six three percent.","三元模型的输出是：They also point to ninety nine point six billion dollars from two hundred four oh six three percent。"],[20.17,22.89,61,"It sounds much more like real financial news.","这听起来更像真实的财经新闻了。"],[23.24,26.69,61,"So as N grows, the text becomes more fluent.","所以随着 N 增大，文本变得更流畅。"],[27.94,33.36,62,"And here is the same experiment on Shakespeare, from unigram up to four-gram.","这是在莎士比亚作品上做的同样实验，从一元一直到四元。"],[33.71,40.8,62,"At four-gram, the text sounds really like Shakespeare: King Henry. What! I will go seek the traitor Gloucester.","到了四元，文本听起来真的很像莎士比亚：King Henry. What! I will go seek the traitor Gloucester."],[41.15,47.66,62,"But be careful: many of these four-gram sentences are copied almost directly from the original plays.","但要小心：这些四元模型生成的句子，很多几乎是直接从原剧本里照搬的。"],[48.91,53.92,63,"So here is the next question: what is the drawback of a large N?","那么下一个问题是：N 很大有什么缺点？"],[54.27,62.35,63,"First, space complexity: the number of possible n-grams grows exponentially with N, so we need a lot of memory.","第一，空间复杂度：可能的 n-gram 数量随 N 指数增长，所以需要大量内存。"],[62.7,69.73,63,"Second, less diversity: with large N, the model mostly memorizes and copies the training text.","第二，多样性下降：N 很大时，模型主要是在记忆和照抄训练文本。"],[70.08,74.55,63,"And there is a third problem, sparsity, which we'll see right now.","还有第三个问题，稀疏性，我们马上就会看到。"],[75.8,77.23,64,"Let's look at the numbers.","我们来看数字。"],[77.58,85.71,64,"The Shakespeare corpus has about eight hundred eighty-five thousand word tokens, and a vocabulary of about twenty-nine thousand word types.","莎士比亚语料大约有 88.5 万个词例，词表大约有 2.9 万个词型。"],[86.06,94.53,64,"Shakespeare produced about three hundred thousand bigram types, out of V squared, which is about eight hundred forty-four million possible bigrams.","莎士比亚用到了大约 30 万种二元组，而可能的二元组有 V 的平方，约 8.44 亿种。"],[94.88,100.75,64,"So ninety-nine point nine six percent of possible bigrams never appear in the corpus.","所以 99.96% 的可能二元组从未在语料中出现。"],[101.1,107.79,64,"Our relative frequency estimate gives non-zero probability to only zero point zero four percent of them.","我们的相对频率估计只给其中 0.04% 的二元组赋予了非零概率。"],[108.14,111.07,64,"For trigrams and four-grams it's even worse.","对三元组和四元组来说情况更糟。"],[111.42,116.76,64,"If you want to try this yourself, the tiny Shakespeare dataset is linked at the bottom of the slide.","如果你想自己试试，幻灯片底部给出了 tiny Shakespeare 数据集的链接。"],[118.01,122.39,65,"This is the core problem: MLE does not capture unseen events.","这就是核心问题：MLE 无法处理未见过的事件。"],[122.74,127.22,65,"Suppose we estimated a model on four hundred forty thousand word tokens.","假设我们在 44 万个词例上估计了一个模型。"],[127.57,135.17,65,"Only thirty thousand word types occur in the training data, and any word that does not appear has zero probability.","训练数据中只出现了 3 万个词型，任何没出现过的词概率都是零。"],[135.52,143.76,65,"Only zero point zero four percent of all possible bigrams occur, and any bigram that does not appear has zero probability.","所有可能的二元组中只有 0.04% 出现过，任何没出现过的二元组概率都是零。"],[144.11,147.97,65,"That is true even if we have seen both words separately.","即使这两个词我们都分别见过，也是如此。"],[148.32,154.53,65,"And if a single bigram in a test sentence has zero probability, the whole sentence gets probability zero.","而如果测试句子中有一个二元组概率为零，整个句子的概率就成了零。"],[154.88,156.29,65,"That is clearly wrong.","这显然是不对的。"],[157.54,159.67,66,"The solution is called smoothing.","解决办法叫作平滑。"],[160.02,167.04,66,"We take a little probability mass away from the events we have seen, and give it to the events we have not seen.","我们从见过的事件那里拿走一点概率质量，分给没见过的事件。"],[167.39,171.93,66,"In the MLE model, the seen events have total probability one.","在 MLE 模型中，见过的事件总概率为 1。"],[172.28,179.53,66,"In the smoothed model, the seen events have probability a bit less than one, and the unseen events get something larger than zero.","在平滑后的模型中，见过的事件概率略小于 1，未见过的事件则得到大于 0 的概率。"],[179.88,187.42,66,"We won't spend much time on smoothing this year, because neural models handle this more naturally, but you should know the main ideas.","今年我们不会在平滑上花太多时间，因为神经模型能更自然地处理这个问题，但你们应该了解主要思想。"],[188.67,190.88,67,"Here are the main smoothing methods.","这里是主要的平滑方法。"],[191.23,196.45,67,"Add-one smoothing: pretend every event happened one more time than it actually did.","加一平滑：假装每个事件都比实际多发生了一次。"],[196.8,203.49,67,"Linear interpolation: mix the n-gram model with the n minus one gram model, using a weight lambda.","线性插值：用权重 λ 把 n 元模型和 n−1 元模型混合起来。"],[203.84,209.7,67,"Absolute discounting: subtract a constant count from frequent events and give it to rare events.","绝对折扣：从高频事件中减去一个固定计数，分给低频事件。"],[210.05,217.09,67,"And Kneser-Ney, which is absolute discounting with a smarter unigram distribution.","还有 Kneser-Ney，它是采用了更聪明的一元分布的绝对折扣。"],[217.44,223.31,67,"Kneser-Ney was the standard for many years in speech recognition and machine translation.","Kneser-Ney 多年来一直是语音识别和机器翻译中的标准方法。"],[224.56,233.98,68,"Add-one, or Laplace smoothing, is the simplest: add one, or some small k, to the count of every possible event.","加一平滑，也叫拉普拉斯平滑，是最简单的：给每个可能事件的计数加 1，或者加一个小的 k。"],[234.33,236.81,68,"But look at what happens with Shakespeare.","但看看在莎士比亚语料上会发生什么。"],[237.16,245.37,68,"We have about zero point eight eight million real bigram counts, and we add about eight hundred forty-four million fake counts.","我们有大约 88 万个真实的二元组计数，却加上了大约 8.44 亿个虚构的计数。"],[245.72,252.61,68,"So almost none of the probability mass comes from real data anymore. We are back to word salad.","于是几乎没有概率质量再来自真实数据了。我们又回到了词语沙拉。"],[252.96,257.69,68,"So k needs to be really small, and even then it doesn't work very well.","所以 k 必须非常小，即便如此效果也不太好。"],[258.94,263.63,69,"Now, once we have built a language model, how do we know whether it is any good?","那么，建好一个语言模型之后，我们怎么知道它好不好？"],[263.98,265.76,69,"This brings us to evaluation.","这就引出了评测。"],[267.01,272.32,70,"How do we know whether one language model is better than another? There are two ways.","怎么知道一个语言模型比另一个好？有两种方式。"],[272.67,281.58,70,"Intrinsic evaluation measures how well the model captures what it is supposed to capture, for example, the probability of real text.","内在评测衡量模型对它应该刻画的东西刻画得有多好，比如真实文本的概率。"],[281.93,291.58,70,"Extrinsic, or task-based, evaluation measures how useful the model is for a particular task, such as speech recognition or translation.","外在评测，也叫基于任务的评测，衡量模型在特定任务上有多有用，比如语音识别或翻译。"],[291.93,297.03,70,"In both cases, we need an evaluation metric to compare different models.","两种情况都需要一个评测指标来比较不同的模型。"],[298.28,302.78,71,"The standard intrinsic metric for language models is perplexity.","语言模型的标准内在评测指标是困惑度。"],[303.13,306.63,71,"You will see this word in almost every language model paper.","几乎每一篇语言模型论文里你都会看到这个词。"],[307.88,314.74,72,"The perplexity of a language model is the inverse of the probability of the test set, normalized by the number of tokens N.","语言模型的困惑度，是测试集概率的倒数，再按词元数 N 做归一化。"],[315.09,321.07,72,"In the formula, it's the N-th root of one over the probability of the test set.","在公式里，它就是测试集概率倒数的 N 次方根。"],[321.42,328.71,72,"A model with lower perplexity is better, because it assigns a higher probability to unseen test text.","困惑度越低的模型越好，因为它给未见过的测试文本赋予了更高的概率。"],[329.06,339.83,72,"Intuitively, a perplexity of one hundred means the model is as confused as if it had to choose uniformly among one hundred words at each step.","直观地说，困惑度为 100，意味着模型的困惑程度，相当于每一步都要在 100 个词中均匀地随机选一个。"],[340.18,345.9,72,"Two notes. First, you can only compare perplexities if the models use the same vocabulary.","有两点说明。第一，只有当模型使用相同的词表时，困惑度才可以比较。"],[346.25,354.31,72,"Second, trigram models usually have lower perplexity than bigram models, and bigram models lower than unigram models.","第二，三元模型的困惑度通常低于二元模型，二元模型又低于一元模型。"],[355.56,360.07,73,"A practical issue: language model probabilities are very small.","一个实际问题：语言模型的概率都非常小。"],[360.42,367.96,73,"If we multiply many of them together, the number becomes so small that the computer rounds it to zero. This is called underflow.","如果把很多概率乘在一起，数值会小到计算机把它舍入为零。这叫下溢。"],[368.31,370.81,73,"So in practice we use logarithms.","所以在实践中我们使用对数。"],[371.16,377.4,73,"Instead of multiplying probabilities, we sum log probabilities, and then take the exponential at the end.","不再把概率相乘，而是把对数概率相加，最后再取指数。"],[377.75,381.28,73,"This is the formula you will actually implement in your assignment.","这就是你们在作业中实际要实现的公式。"],[382.53,387.07,74,"Now let's look at extrinsic, or task-based, evaluation.","现在来看外在评测，也就是基于任务的评测。"],[387.42,392.4,74,"The classic example is word error rate, WER, used in speech recognition.","经典的例子是语音识别中使用的词错误率，WER。"],[393.65,398.36,75,"Perplexity tells us which model assigns higher probability to unseen text.","困惑度告诉我们哪个模型给未见文本赋予了更高的概率。"],[398.71,406.09,75,"But that does not necessarily tell us which model is better for our actual task, such as scoring candidate sentences.","但这并不一定能告诉我们哪个模型在实际任务上更好，比如给候选句子打分。"],[406.44,412.97,75,"In task-based evaluation, we train model A, plug it into our system for task T, and measure the performance.","在基于任务的评测中，我们训练模型 A，把它接入执行任务 T 的系统，然后测量性能。"],[413.32,416.7,75,"Then we do the same with model B on the same task.","然后对模型 B 在同一任务上做同样的事。"],[417.05,420.56,75,"Finally, we compare the scores of the two systems.","最后比较两个系统的得分。"],[421.81,424.45,76,"How do language models help downstream tasks?","语言模型怎样帮助下游任务？"],[424.8,431.6,76,"First, we can use a language model as a random sentence generator, sampling sentences according to their probability.","第一，可以把语言模型当作随机句子生成器，按照句子的概率来采样。"],[431.95,440.12,76,"Second, systems like machine translation, speech recognition, spell-checking and generation often produce many candidate outputs.","第二，机器翻译、语音识别、拼写检查和文本生成等系统，常常会产生多个候选输出。"],[440.47,446.92,76,"We prefer outputs with higher probability, so we use the language model to score and rank the candidates.","我们更偏好概率更高的输出，所以用语言模型给候选结果打分和排序。"],[447.27,453.99,76,"Using Bayes' rule, the best output maximizes P of the input given the output, times P of the output.","用贝叶斯公式，最优输出是让“给定输出时输入的概率”乘以“输出的概率”最大的那个。"],[454.34,457.47,76,"The second term is exactly the language model.","第二项正是语言模型。"],[457.82,461.67,76,"The same idea is also used in information retrieval.","同样的思想也用在信息检索中。"],[462.92,466.03,77,"Here's a concrete example from speech recognition.","这是语音识别中的一个具体例子。"],[466.38,470.75,77,"Someone says, in Chinese: there is a Jay Chou concert here.","有人用中文说：这儿有周杰伦演唱会。"],[471.1,473.43,77,"The acoustic model only hears the sounds.","声学模型只能听到声音。"],[473.78,479.94,77,"It can't tell which characters are meant, because several different written forms of the name sound exactly the same.","它分不清是哪几个字，因为这个名字有好几种写法，读音完全一样，比如周杰轮、周捷伦。"],[480.29,487.33,77,"But when we add a language model, it knows that the real name, Jay Chou, is by far the most common string.","但加上语言模型后，它知道真正的名字“周杰伦”是到目前为止最常见的写法。"],[487.68,490.63,77,"So the combined system gets the right answer.","所以组合后的系统得到了正确答案。"],[491.88,498.06,78,"Word error rate, WER, was originally developed for speech recognition.","词错误率 WER 最初是为语音识别设计的。"],[498.41,503.58,78,"It measures how much the predicted word sequence differs from the correct transcript.","它衡量预测的词序列和正确转写之间相差多少。"],[503.93,506.01,78,"We count three kinds of errors.","我们统计三类错误。"],[506.36,510.14,78,"Insertions, like eat lunch becoming eat a lunch.","插入，比如 eat lunch 变成了 eat a lunch。"],[510.49,514.22,78,"Deletions, like see a movie becoming see movie.","删除，比如 see a movie 变成了 see movie。"],[514.57,518.85,78,"And substitutions, like drink ice tea becoming drink nice tea.","还有替换，比如 drink ice tea 变成了 drink nice tea。"],[519.2,528.17,78,"WER is the total number of insertions, deletions and substitutions, divided by the number of words in the correct transcript.","WER 等于插入、删除和替换的总数，除以正确转写中的词数。"],[528.52,530.14,78,"Lower is better.","越低越好。"],[531.39,534.41,79,"Here is another example: machine translation.","再看另一个例子：机器翻译。"],[534.76,539.76,79,"We want to translate the English sentence, I think therefore I am, into Chinese.","我们想把英文句子 I think therefore I am 翻译成中文。"],[540.11,543.69,79,"A translation system might give us several candidates.","一个翻译系统可能会给出好几个候选译文。"],[544.04,552.61,79,"They range from the classic four-character form, wo si gu wo zai, and the same phrase with punctuation, to longer and more literal versions.","从经典的四字形式“我思故我在”，到加了标点的同一句话，再到更长、更直译的版本。"],[552.96,564.94,79,"For instance, wo si kao, suo yi wo cun zai, which means I think, so I exist; a terse one, si, ze zai; and a loose one, thinking makes me who I am.","比如“我思考，所以我存在”；简短的“思，则在”；还有比较意译的“思考让我成为‘我’”。"],[565.29,567.16,79,"So which one is better?","那么，哪一个更好呢？"],[568.41,570.37,80,"We can let the language model decide.","我们可以让语言模型来做决定。"],[570.72,575.06,80,"It gives each candidate a score: high, middle-high, or low.","它给每个候选打一个分数：高、中高或低。"],[575.41,583.36,80,"For example, wo si gu wo zai gets the highest probability, because it is the most common and fluent form in Chinese text.","比如，“我思故我在”得到最高的概率，因为它是中文文本里最常见、最通顺的说法。"],[583.71,589.15,80,"But notice: the most fluent translation is not necessarily the best translation.","但请注意：最通顺的译文不一定是最好的译文。"],[589.5,596.11,80,"Chinese translators talk about xin, da and ya: faithfulness, fluency and elegance.","中国的翻译家讲究“信、达、雅”：忠实、通顺、优雅。"],[596.46,600.65,80,"A language model mainly measures fluency, that is, the da part.","语言模型主要衡量的是通顺程度，也就是“达”。"],[601.9,606.25,81,"Let me recap what we have covered in the narrow-sense part.","我们来回顾一下狭义语言模型这一部分讲过的内容。"],[607.5,613.63,82,"The key concepts are: n-gram language models, and the independence, or Markov, assumptions behind them.","关键概念有：n-gram 语言模型，以及它背后的独立性假设，也就是马尔可夫假设。"],[613.98,620.97,82,"How to go from n-grams to a proper distribution over a language, using the BOS and EOS symbols.","如何借助 BOS 和 EOS 符号，从 n-gram 得到一个定义在整个语言上的合法概率分布。"],[621.32,626.62,82,"Relative frequency, or maximum likelihood, estimation; and smoothing.","相对频率估计，也就是最大似然估计；以及平滑。"],[626.97,634.39,82,"And evaluation, both intrinsic, with perplexity, and extrinsic, with word error rate.","还有评估：内在评估用困惑度，外在评估用词错误率。"],[634.74,639.67,82,"If you understand these, you understand the foundations of every modern language model.","理解了这些，你就理解了所有现代语言模型的基础。"]]},{"n":6,"title":"From N-grams to Neural LMs and Word Vectors","blurb":"The sparsity and storage problems of n-grams, the fixed-window neural LM, word2vec's context-based word vectors, and subword models such as MorphTE.","file":"lecture03_part6.mp4","poster":"lecture03_part6.jpg","duration":548.8,"first":83,"last":98,"slides":[{"n":83,"title":"Today’s Lecture","t":0.8},{"n":84,"title":"the students opened their Question: How to learn a Language Model? Answer (pre- Deep Learning): learn an n-gram Language","t":12.69},{"n":85,"title":"N-gram Language Models","t":63.73},{"n":86,"title":"N-gram Language Models: Example","t":90.64},{"n":87,"title":"Sparsity Problems with N-gram Language Models","t":142.94},{"n":88,"title":"Storage Problems with N-gram Language Models","t":181.31},{"n":89,"title":"How to Build a Neural Language Model?","t":201.03},{"n":90,"title":"A Fixed-window Neural Language Model","t":229.09},{"n":91,"title":"A Fixed-window Neural Language Model","t":261.89},{"n":92,"title":"From N-gram LMs to Word Vectors","t":311.34},{"n":93,"title":"the students","t":318.11},{"n":94,"title":"Representing Words by Their Context","t":343.67},{"n":95,"title":"Word2vec: Objective Function","t":387.21},{"n":96,"title":"Word Structure and Subword Models","t":435.65},{"n":97,"title":"Interesting Characters/Words","t":474.39},{"n":98,"title":"A Paper from Our Group: MorphTE","t":515.67}],"sents":[[0.8,5.86,83,"Now let's move to the final part: language models in the broad sense.","现在进入最后一部分：广义的语言模型。"],[6.21,11.29,83,"We'll go from n-grams to word2vec, to ELMo and BERT, and beyond.","我们会从 n-gram 讲到 word2vec，再到 ELMo 和 BERT，以及更远。"],[12.54,20.39,84,"Let's quickly revisit n-gram models from a slightly different angle, following the Stanford CS224n presentation.","我们按照斯坦福 CS224n 的讲法，换个角度快速回顾一下 n-gram 模型。"],[20.74,23.77,84,"The question is: how do we learn a language model?","问题是：我们如何学习一个语言模型？"],[24.12,27.72,84,"Before deep learning, the answer was: learn an n-gram model.","在深度学习之前，答案是：学一个 n-gram 模型。"],[28.07,32.9,84,"An n-gram is just a chunk of n consecutive words.","n-gram 就是由 n 个连续的词组成的片段。"],[33.25,41.73,84,"For the phrase, the students opened their, the unigrams are the, students, opened, and their.","对于短语 the students opened their，一元组是 the、students、opened 和 their。"],[42.08,46.62,84,"The bigrams are the students, students opened, and opened their.","二元组是 the students、students opened 和 opened their。"],[46.97,53.92,84,"The trigrams are the students opened, and students opened their; and the four-gram is the whole phrase.","三元组是 the students opened 和 students opened their；四元组就是整个短语。"],[54.27,62.33,84,"The idea is to collect statistics about how frequent different n-grams are, and use them to predict the next word.","思路是统计各种 n-gram 出现的频率，并用它们来预测下一个词。"],[63.58,71.56,85,"First, we make the Markov assumption: the next word depends only on the preceding n minus one words.","首先，我们做马尔可夫假设：下一个词只依赖于前面 n 减 1 个词。"],[71.91,81.53,85,"Then, by the definition of conditional probability, this equals the probability of the n-gram divided by the probability of the n minus one gram.","然后根据条件概率的定义，它等于 n-gram 的概率除以 n 减 1 元组的概率。"],[81.88,83.56,85,"How do we get these probabilities?","这些概率从哪里来？"],[83.91,86.2,85,"By counting them in a large corpus.","在一个大型语料库里数出来。"],[86.55,89.24,85,"This is a statistical approximation.","这是一种统计近似。"],[90.49,94.21,86,"Here's an example. Suppose we are learning a four-gram model.","来看一个例子。假设我们在学习一个四元语言模型。"],[94.56,99.97,86,"The text is: as the proctor started the clock, the students opened their, blank.","文本是：as the proctor started the clock, the students opened their，后面是空格。"],[100.32,107.76,86,"A four-gram model throws away everything except the last three words, students opened their, and conditions only on those.","四元模型会丢掉除最后三个词之外的所有内容，只以 students opened their 为条件。"],[108.11,114.1,86,"Suppose in our corpus, students opened their occurred one thousand times.","假设在语料库中，students opened their 出现了一千次。"],[114.45,121.4,86,"Students opened their books occurred four hundred times, so the probability of books is zero point four.","students opened their books 出现了四百次，所以 books 的概率是 0.4。"],[121.75,128.82,86,"Students opened their exams occurred one hundred times, so the probability of exams is zero point one.","students opened their exams 出现了一百次，所以 exams 的概率是 0.1。"],[129.17,133.23,86,"But wait: the proctor and the clock suggest an exam!","但是等一下：监考老师和计时钟都暗示这是一场考试！"],[133.58,137.36,86,"By discarding that context, the model makes the wrong guess.","丢掉了这段上下文，模型就猜错了。"],[137.71,141.54,86,"This shows the limitation of a fixed, short context.","这说明了固定的短上下文的局限。"],[142.79,145.03,87,"Now, the sparsity problems.","接下来是稀疏性问题。"],[145.38,152.42,87,"Problem one: what if students opened their w never occurred in the data? Then w gets probability zero.","问题一：如果 students opened their w 在数据里从没出现过呢？那 w 的概率就是零。"],[152.77,157.71,87,"The partial solution is to add a small delta to every count. That's smoothing.","部分的解决办法是给每个计数加上一个小的 delta。这就是平滑。"],[158.06,166.01,87,"Problem two: what if students opened their itself never occurred? Then we can't compute the probability for any word at all.","问题二：如果 students opened their 本身就没出现过呢？那我们根本无法计算任何词的概率。"],[166.36,172.43,87,"The partial solution is to condition on a shorter context, just opened their. This is called backoff.","部分的解决办法是改用更短的上下文，只看 opened their。这叫做回退。"],[172.78,179.91,87,"And note: increasing n makes sparsity worse. In practice, n is rarely bigger than five.","还要注意：n 越大，稀疏问题越严重。实际中 n 很少超过 5。"],[181.16,183.22,88,"There is also a storage problem.","还有一个存储问题。"],[183.57,186.98,88,"We need to store counts for all n-grams we saw in the corpus.","我们需要存储语料中见过的所有 n-gram 的计数。"],[187.33,192.51,88,"If we increase n, or increase the corpus, the model size grows quickly.","如果增大 n，或者增大语料，模型规模会迅速膨胀。"],[192.86,199.63,88,"So n-gram models suffer from both sparsity and storage problems. This motivates neural language models.","所以 n-gram 模型同时受困于稀疏和存储问题。这就催生了神经语言模型。"],[200.88,203.31,89,"So how do we build a neural language model?","那么，如何构建一个神经语言模型呢？"],[203.66,211.78,89,"Recall the task: the input is a sequence of words, and the output is a probability distribution over the next word.","回顾一下任务：输入是一串词，输出是下一个词的概率分布。"],[212.13,215.22,89,"One simple idea is a window-based neural model.","一个简单的想法是基于窗口的神经模型。"],[215.57,219.84,89,"You may remember a similar model used for named entity recognition.","你可能还记得，命名实体识别中用过类似的模型。"],[220.19,227.69,89,"We take a window of words, like museums in Paris are amazing, and classify the center word, Paris, as a location.","我们取一个词窗口，比如 museums in Paris are amazing，把中心词 Paris 分类为地点。"],[228.94,231.48,90,"Here is the fixed-window neural language model.","这就是固定窗口的神经语言模型。"],[231.83,237.91,90,"From the bottom: we take the last four words, the students opened their, as one-hot vectors.","从底部开始：我们把最后四个词 the students opened their 表示成 one-hot 向量。"],[238.26,242.62,90,"We look up their word embeddings and concatenate them into one vector, e.","查出它们的词向量，再拼接成一个向量 e。"],[242.97,248.35,90,"Then a hidden layer: h equals f of W e plus b one.","然后是隐藏层：h 等于 f 作用于 W 乘 e 加 b1。"],[248.7,255.43,90,"And finally an output layer with softmax, which gives a probability distribution over the whole vocabulary.","最后是带 softmax 的输出层，给出整个词表上的概率分布。"],[255.78,260.49,90,"Here, words like books and laptops get high probability.","在这里，books 和 laptops 这样的词得到了较高的概率。"],[261.74,268.75,91,"This is roughly the model from Bengio and colleagues in two thousand three, A Neural Probabilistic Language Model.","这大致就是 Bengio 等人 2003 年提出的模型，《A Neural Probabilistic Language Model》。"],[269.1,275.51,91,"The improvements over n-grams: no sparsity problem, because similar words have similar embeddings.","相比 n-gram 的改进：没有稀疏问题，因为相似的词有相似的词向量。"],[275.86,279.23,91,"And we don't need to store all observed n-grams.","而且不需要存储所有见过的 n-gram。"],[279.58,283.73,91,"But there are remaining problems. The fixed window is too small.","但仍然有问题。固定窗口太小了。"],[284.08,290.4,91,"If we enlarge the window, the matrix W grows. And the window can never be large enough.","如果扩大窗口，矩阵 W 也会变大。而且窗口永远不可能足够大。"],[290.75,299.25,91,"Also, each position is multiplied by completely different weights in W, so there is no symmetry in how the inputs are processed.","另外，每个位置乘的是 W 中完全不同的权重，所以处理输入的方式没有对称性。"],[299.6,303.93,91,"We need an architecture that can process inputs of any length.","我们需要一种能处理任意长度输入的架构。"],[304.28,309.94,91,"Recurrent neural networks were the first answer, and later, Transformers.","循环神经网络是第一个答案，后来则是 Transformer。"],[311.19,316.71,92,"Now an important side story: from n-gram language models to word vectors.","下面是一段重要的插曲：从 n-gram 语言模型到词向量。"],[317.96,322.5,93,"Here's an interesting by-product of the neural language model.","这是神经语言模型一个有趣的副产品。"],[322.85,329.8,93,"To predict the next word, the model must learn an embedding for every word, the vectors at the bottom of this picture.","为了预测下一个词，模型必须为每个词学一个词向量，也就是图中底部的这些向量。"],[330.15,333.43,93,"These embeddings turn out to be very useful by themselves.","这些词向量本身就非常有用。"],[333.78,337.43,93,"Words with similar meanings end up with similar vectors.","意思相近的词，最终会得到相近的向量。"],[337.78,342.27,93,"This is where word embeddings, or word vectors, come from.","这就是词嵌入，也就是词向量的由来。"],[343.52,353.25,94,"The idea behind word vectors is distributional semantics: a word's meaning is given by the words that frequently appear close by.","词向量背后的思想是分布式语义：一个词的意思由经常出现在它附近的词决定。"],[353.6,359.48,94,"As J. R. Firth said in nineteen fifty-seven: you shall know a word by the company it keeps.","正如 J. R. Firth 在 1957 年所说：观其伴，知其义。"],[359.83,364.23,94,"This is one of the most successful ideas in modern statistical NLP.","这是现代统计自然语言处理中最成功的思想之一。"],[364.58,371.67,94,"When a word w appears in a text, its context is the set of words nearby, within a fixed window.","当词 w 出现在文本中时，它的上下文就是固定窗口内附近的那些词。"],[372.02,376.33,94,"We use many contexts of w to build its representation.","我们用 w 的许多上下文来构建它的表示。"],[376.68,385.81,94,"For example, all these contexts around banking, like government debt problems, crises, and regulation, together represent the meaning of banking.","比如，banking 周围的这些上下文，像政府债务问题、危机、监管，共同表示了 banking 的含义。"],[387.06,391.09,95,"Word2vec turns this idea into an objective function.","Word2vec 把这个想法变成了一个目标函数。"],[391.44,399.11,95,"We want to minimize the negative average log probability of each context word given the center word, over all positions in the text.","我们要最小化：在文本所有位置上，给定中心词时每个上下文词的平均负对数概率。"],[399.46,405.06,95,"How do we compute the probability of a context word o, given a center word c?","给定中心词 c，如何计算上下文词 o 的概率？"],[405.41,411.66,95,"We use two vectors for each word: v, when it's a center word, and u, when it's a context word.","每个词用两个向量：作为中心词时用 v，作为上下文词时用 u。"],[412.01,420.19,95,"Then we take the dot product of u o and v c, and apply a softmax over the vocabulary.","然后计算 u_o 和 v_c 的点积，再在整个词表上做 softmax。"],[420.54,428.27,95,"It's called max because it amplifies the largest values, and soft because it still gives some probability to smaller ones.","叫 max 是因为它放大最大的值；叫 soft 是因为它仍给较小的值留一些概率。"],[428.62,434.25,95,"In a sense, word2vec is a very simple language model: predict nearby words.","从某种意义上说，word2vec 是一个非常简单的语言模型：预测附近的词。"],[435.5,441.96,96,"So far, we have assumed a fixed vocabulary of tens of thousands of words, built from the training set.","到目前为止，我们一直假设有一个从训练集构建的、几万个词的固定词表。"],[442.31,445.23,96,"All new words at test time are mapped to UNK.","测试时出现的所有新词都被映射成 UNK。"],[445.58,449.64,96,"Look at the table: common words like hat and learn are fine.","看这张表：hat 和 learn 这样的常见词没问题。"],[449.99,457.89,96,"But variations like taaaaasty, misspellings like laern, and novel words like Transformerify all become UNK.","但像 taaaaasty 这样的变体、laern 这样的拼写错误、Transformerify 这样的新词，都变成了 UNK。"],[458.24,465.95,96,"This makes even less sense for languages with rich morphology, where there are many more word types, and each appears fewer times.","对于形态丰富的语言，这就更不合理了：词形多得多，每个词出现的次数也更少。"],[466.3,472.99,96,"The modern solution is subword models, such as byte-pair encoding, which you will see in later lectures.","现代的解决方案是子词模型，比如字节对编码 BPE，后面的课会讲到。"],[474.24,476.59,97,"Here are some interesting examples.","来看几个有趣的例子。"],[476.94,485.54,97,"First, a rare Chinese character, pronounced yan, third tone, or tao, first tone, meaning big on top and small at the bottom.","第一个是生僻字“夵”，读 yǎn 或 tāo，意思是上大下小。"],[485.89,487.56,97,"Most models have never seen it.","大多数模型从没见过它。"],[487.91,496.22,97,"Second, LGUer, a word our students invented, meaning a student of CUHK-Shenzhen. It is not in any dictionary.","第二个是 LGUer，这是我们学生自创的词，意思是港中大（深圳）的学生。任何词典里都没有它。"],[496.57,504.91,97,"Third, Looong, with extra o's. Our own group even used this spelling in a paper title, looongLLaVA.","第三个是 Looong，多了几个 o。我们组甚至在论文标题 looongLLaVA 里用了这种拼法。"],[505.26,507.43,97,"How should a model handle words like these?","模型该如何处理这样的词呢？"],[507.78,514.27,97,"Subword tokenization helps here, because it can split them into smaller, known pieces.","子词切分在这里很有帮助，因为它能把它们拆成更小的、已知的片段。"],[515.52,521.25,98,"Here is a paper from our group, MorphTE, published at NeurIPS two thousand twenty-two.","这是我们组的一篇论文 MorphTE，发表在 NeurIPS 2022。"],[521.6,525.53,98,"The idea is to inject morphology into word embeddings.","它的思路是把形态学信息注入词向量。"],[525.88,535.47,98,"We split each word into morphemes, like kind and ness for kindness, and build the word embedding from morpheme embeddings using tensor products.","我们把每个词切分成语素，比如 kindness 切成 kind 和 ness，再用张量积由语素向量构建词向量。"],[535.82,542.75,98,"This makes the embedding layer much smaller, while keeping, or even improving, performance.","这让嵌入层小得多，同时保持甚至提升了性能。"],[543.1,546.95,98,"If you are interested in this direction, feel free to reach out to me.","如果你对这个方向感兴趣，欢迎来找我交流。"]]},{"n":7,"title":"Contextualized Word Vectors: ELMo and BERT","blurb":"Why static vectors fall short, how ELMo and BERT produce contextual embeddings, and BERT's masked LM and next sentence prediction, step by step.","file":"lecture03_part7.mp4","poster":"lecture03_part7.jpg","duration":893.8,"first":99,"last":123,"slides":[{"n":99,"title":"From Static Word Vector to Contextualized Word Vectors","t":0.8},{"n":100,"title":"What’s Wrong with Word2vec?","t":7.32},{"n":101,"title":"Contextualized Word Embeddings","t":37.64},{"n":102,"title":"ELMo","t":61.88},{"n":103,"title":"ELMo","t":90.66},{"n":104,"title":"How to Use ELMo?","t":113.51},{"n":105,"title":"Use ELMo in Practice","t":152.82},{"n":106,"title":"BERT","t":172.01},{"n":107,"title":"Bidirectional Encoders","t":215.8},{"n":108,"title":"Bidirectional Encoders","t":252.41},{"n":109,"title":"Objective I: Masked Language Models (MLMs)","t":280.42},{"n":110,"title":"A little more complication:","t":310.04},{"n":111,"title":"Pretraining Encoders: What Pretraining Objective to Use?","t":345.7},{"n":112,"title":"Objective II: Next Sentence Prediction (NSP)","t":377.75},{"n":113,"title":"On BERT Pre-training","t":414.76},{"n":114,"title":"Directionality Helps","t":443.07},{"n":115,"title":"Pre-training and Fine-tuning","t":466.09},{"n":116,"title":"More Details","t":495.7},{"n":117,"title":"BERT Step 1: Building a Training Example","t":534.7},{"n":118,"title":"BERT Step 2: Pre-training (Masked LM + NSP)","t":632.3},{"n":119,"title":"BERT Step 3: Inference and Fine-tuning","t":717.27},{"n":120,"title":"One for All","t":796.72},{"n":121,"title":"Applications","t":820.43},{"n":122,"title":"Scalability: BERT","t":846.68},{"n":123,"title":"Scalability: GPT","t":861.21}],"sents":[[0.8,5.92,99,"Next step: from static word vectors to contextualized word vectors.","下一步：从静态词向量到上下文相关的词向量。"],[7.17,9.13,100,"What's wrong with word2vec?","word2vec 有什么问题？"],[9.48,15.61,100,"It gives one vector for each word type. For example, bank always has the same vector.","它给每个词型只分配一个向量。比如 bank 永远是同一个向量。"],[15.96,22.08,100,"But word use is complex: it has semantics, syntactic behavior, and connotations.","但词的用法很复杂：有语义、句法行为，还有隐含意义。"],[22.43,24.46,100,"And many words are polysemous.","而且很多词是多义词。"],[24.81,28.06,100,"Mouse can be a computer device or a small animal.","mouse 可以是电脑鼠标，也可以是一种小动物。"],[28.41,32.79,100,"Bank can be a financial institution or the side of a river.","bank 可以是金融机构，也可以是河岸。"],[33.14,36.24,100,"A single vector can't capture all these meanings.","单个向量无法涵盖所有这些含义。"],[37.49,42.47,101,"So the idea is: let's build a vector for each word conditioned on its context.","所以思路是：让每个词的向量都以它的上下文为条件。"],[42.82,51.55,101,"We want a function f that takes the whole sentence, w one to w n, and outputs a vector for each word, x one to x n.","我们想要一个函数 f，输入整句话 w1 到 wn，为每个词输出一个向量 x1 到 xn。"],[51.9,56.78,101,"Now bank in river bank, and bank in bank account, will have different vectors.","这样，river bank 里的 bank 和 bank account 里的 bank 就会有不同的向量。"],[57.13,60.48,101,"These are called contextualized word embeddings.","这就叫做上下文相关的词嵌入。"],[61.73,70.64,102,"The first famous model of this kind is ELMo, from NAACL two thousand eighteen: Deep contextualized word representations.","这类模型第一个出名的是 ELMo，来自 NAACL 2018 的论文《Deep contextualized word representations》。"],[70.99,76.15,102,"The key idea is simple: train an LSTM-based language model on a large corpus.","核心思想很简单：在大规模语料上训练一个基于 LSTM 的语言模型。"],[76.5,82.62,102,"Then use the hidden states of the LSTM for each token as that token's representation.","然后用 LSTM 在每个词上的隐藏状态作为这个词的表示。"],[82.97,89.26,102,"And yes, it's named after the Sesame Street character, which started a whole tradition of model names.","没错，它的名字来自《芝麻街》里的角色，由此开启了一整套模型命名的传统。"],[90.51,91.85,103,"Here's the architecture.","这是它的架构。"],[92.2,99.76,103,"ELMo has two language models: a forward one, reading left to right, and a backward one, reading right to left.","ELMo 有两个语言模型：一个前向的，从左往右读；一个后向的，从右往左读。"],[100.11,104.22,103,"Each has two LSTM layers on top of the embeddings.","每个模型都在词嵌入之上叠了两层 LSTM。"],[104.57,112.11,103,"The training objective is the sum of the forward log-likelihood and the backward log-likelihood, over all words in the sentence.","训练目标是句子中所有词的前向对数似然与后向对数似然之和。"],[113.36,115.01,104,"How do we use ELMo?","怎么使用 ELMo？"],[115.36,124.79,104,"For each token k, we have a representation from every layer: the input embedding, and the hidden states of each LSTM layer, from both directions.","对每个词 k，每一层都有一个表示：输入嵌入，以及两个方向上每层 LSTM 的隐藏状态。"],[125.14,128.17,104,"The final ELMo vector is a weighted sum of all layers.","最终的 ELMo 向量是所有层的加权和。"],[128.52,135.66,104,"The weights s are softmax-normalized, and gamma scales the whole vector. Both are learned for each task.","权重 s 经过 softmax 归一化，gamma 对整个向量做缩放。两者都针对每个任务学习。"],[136.01,143.51,104,"In practice, we freeze all the language model weights, and just plug the ELMo vector into any neural NLP model.","实际使用时，我们冻结语言模型的所有权重，直接把 ELMo 向量接入任意神经 NLP 模型。"],[143.86,151.42,104,"We do this by concatenating it with the input representation. You can also insert it into higher layers.","做法是把它与输入表示拼接起来。也可以把它接到更高的层。"],[152.67,159.38,105,"In practice, you can use pre-trained ELMo from AllenNLP, and it's also available in TensorFlow.","实践中，可以直接用 AllenNLP 提供的预训练 ELMo，TensorFlow 里也有。"],[159.73,164.98,105,"The table lists the pre-trained models, from the small one up to the original size.","表格列出了各个预训练模型，从小模型到原始大小的模型。"],[165.33,170.61,105,"With just a few lines of code, you can turn sentences into contextualized embeddings.","只需几行代码，就能把句子转换成上下文相关的词向量。"],[171.86,179.71,106,"Then came BERT, released in October two thousand eighteen, and published at NAACL two thousand nineteen.","接着是 BERT，2018 年 10 月发布，发表于 NAACL 2019。"],[180.06,186.08,106,"Its title: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding.","论文题目是《Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding》。"],[186.43,189.5,106,"How is BERT different from ELMo? Three points.","BERT 和 ELMo 有什么不同？有三点。"],[189.85,197.21,106,"One: ELMo uses two separate unidirectional contexts, while BERT uses truly bidirectional context.","第一：ELMo 用的是两个分开的单向上下文，而 BERT 用的是真正的双向上下文。"],[197.56,204.56,106,"Two: ELMo uses LSTMs, while BERT uses Transformers, which we'll cover in a later lecture.","第二：ELMo 用 LSTM，而 BERT 用 Transformer，后面的课会详细讲。"],[204.91,214.4,106,"Three: in ELMo the weights are frozen, while in BERT all weights are updated on the downstream task. This is called fine-tuning.","第三：ELMo 的权重是冻结的，而 BERT 的所有权重都会在下游任务上更新。这叫做微调。"],[215.65,218.09,107,"Let's talk about bidirectional encoders.","我们来谈谈双向编码器。"],[218.44,222.21,107,"Language models only use left context or right context.","语言模型只用左侧或右侧的上下文。"],[222.56,229.78,107,"ELMo used two independent models, one in each direction, but neither of them sees both sides at the same time.","ELMo 用了两个独立的模型，每个方向一个，但没有哪一个能同时看到两边。"],[230.13,238.83,107,"But language understanding is bidirectional. To understand a word, we often need both what comes before and what comes after.","但语言理解是双向的。要理解一个词，我们往往既需要前文，也需要后文。"],[239.18,245.28,107,"Take terribly in the movie was terribly exciting: its left context and its right context both matter.","以 the movie was terribly exciting 里的 terribly 为例：它的左侧和右侧上下文都很重要。"],[245.63,251.01,107,"So why are language models unidirectional? Think about it for a moment.","那么，语言模型为什么是单向的？请先想一想。"],[252.26,253.27,108,"Here's the reason.","原因在这里。"],[253.62,261.02,108,"If a model could see both directions, then in a deep network, each word could indirectly see itself.","如果模型能同时看两个方向，那么在深层网络里，每个词都可能间接地看到它自己。"],[261.37,266.32,108,"Predicting a word you can already see is trivial, and the model learns nothing.","预测一个已经看得见的词毫无难度，模型什么也学不到。"],[266.67,271.36,108,"On the left, unidirectional context builds the representation incrementally.","左边，单向上下文是逐步构建表示的。"],[271.71,275.92,108,"On the right, with bidirectional context, words can see themselves.","右边，在双向上下文中，词能看到它们自己。"],[276.27,279.02,108,"So we need a different training objective.","所以我们需要一个不同的训练目标。"],[280.27,287.15,109,"BERT's solution is the masked language model: we mask out fifteen percent of the input words, and predict them.","BERT 的解决方案是掩码语言模型：把 15% 的输入词遮住，然后预测它们。"],[287.5,291.47,109,"For example: the man went to the MASK to buy a MASK of milk.","例如：the man went to the [MASK] to buy a [MASK] of milk。"],[291.82,294.3,109,"The model must predict store and gallon.","模型必须预测出 store 和 gallon。"],[294.65,296.06,109,"Why fifteen percent?","为什么是 15%？"],[296.41,303.01,109,"If we mask too little, training is too expensive, because we learn from only a few words per sentence.","遮得太少，训练代价太高，因为每句话只能从很少几个词中学习。"],[303.36,308.64,109,"If we mask too much, there is not enough context left to make predictions.","遮得太多，剩下的上下文又不足以做出预测。"],[309.89,311.81,110,"There is a small complication.","这里有一个小麻烦。"],[312.16,320.95,110,"The MASK token never appears when we use BERT on downstream tasks, so there's a mismatch between pre-training and fine-tuning.","在下游任务中使用 BERT 时，[MASK] 标记从不出现，所以预训练和微调之间存在不匹配。"],[321.3,328.28,110,"To reduce it, for the selected fifteen percent of words, eighty percent of the time we replace the word with MASK.","为了缓解这一点，对选中的 15% 的词，80% 的情况下把它替换成 [MASK]。"],[328.63,333.4,110,"Ten percent of the time, we replace it with a random word, like pizza here.","10% 的情况下替换成一个随机词，比如这里的 pizza。"],[333.75,337.45,110,"And ten percent of the time, we keep it unchanged.","还有 10% 的情况保持不变。"],[337.8,344.3,110,"This way, the model has to build good representations for every word, not just for the masked ones.","这样，模型就必须为每个词都建立良好的表示，而不只是被遮住的词。"],[345.55,350.8,111,"To summarize: encoders get bidirectional context, so we can't do normal language modeling.","总结一下：编码器能看到双向上下文，所以不能做普通的语言建模。"],[351.15,355.03,111,"Instead, we replace some words with MASK and predict them.","取而代之，我们把一些词替换成 [MASK]，然后预测它们。"],[355.38,363.45,111,"Formally, we compute hidden states with the encoder, and predict each masked word with a linear layer and softmax.","形式化地说，用编码器计算隐藏状态，再用线性层加 softmax 预测每个被遮住的词。"],[363.8,366.69,111,"We only add loss terms for the masked words.","只对被遮住的词计算损失。"],[367.04,376.35,111,"If x tilde is the masked version of x, we are learning P of x given x tilde. This is called masked LM.","若 x̃ 是 x 被遮盖后的版本，我们学的就是 P(x | x̃)。这叫做掩码语言模型。"],[377.6,382.75,112,"BERT's second objective is next sentence prediction, or NSP.","BERT 的第二个目标是下一句预测，简称 NSP。"],[383.1,390.05,112,"We sample two sentences, and the model predicts whether the second one really follows the first one, or is a random sentence.","我们采样两个句子，模型预测第二句是否真的紧跟在第一句之后，还是一个随机句子。"],[390.4,397.98,112,"In the first example, the man went to the store, followed by he bought a gallon of milk, is labeled IsNext.","第一个例子里，the man went to the store 后面接 he bought a gallon of milk，标签是 IsNext。"],[398.33,403.41,112,"In the second, penguins are flightless birds does not follow, so it is labeled NotNext.","第二个例子里，penguins are flightless birds 接不上前一句，所以标签是 NotNext。"],[403.76,413.36,112,"However, later papers like SpanBERT and RoBERTa showed that NSP is not really necessary, and removing it can even help.","不过，后来 SpanBERT 和 RoBERTa 等论文表明，NSP 其实并不必要，去掉它甚至可能更好。"],[414.61,417.54,113,"Here's the ablation from the BERT paper.","这是 BERT 论文里的消融实验。"],[417.89,424.16,113,"The masked LM, compared to a left-to-right LM, is very important on some tasks.","与从左到右的语言模型相比，掩码语言模型在某些任务上非常重要。"],[424.51,428.94,113,"And next sentence prediction helps on some others, like QNLI.","而下一句预测在另一些任务上有帮助，比如 QNLI。"],[429.29,437.68,113,"Notice that the left-to-right model does very poorly on SQuAD, a word-level question answering task, because each word cannot see its right context.","注意，从左到右的模型在 SQuAD 这个词级问答任务上表现很差，因为每个词看不到右侧上下文。"],[438.03,441.67,113,"Adding a BiLSTM on top helps a bit.","在上面加一个 BiLSTM 能有所改善。"],[442.92,444.32,114,"Directionality helps.","方向性很有帮助。"],[444.67,450.04,114,"This figure compares the masked LM with the left-to-right LM during pre-training.","这张图比较了预训练过程中掩码语言模型和从左到右语言模型的表现。"],[450.39,459.58,114,"The masked LM converges a bit more slowly, because it only predicts fifteen percent of words per batch, instead of one hundred percent.","掩码语言模型收敛得稍慢一些，因为每批只预测 15% 的词，而不是 100%。"],[459.93,464.69,114,"But its accuracy is much better almost immediately, and stays better.","但它的准确率几乎立刻就高出很多，并且一直保持领先。"],[465.94,469.62,115,"Here's the overall BERT pipeline: pre-training and fine-tuning.","这是 BERT 的整体流程：预训练和微调。"],[469.97,479.28,115,"On the left, we pre-train on unlabeled text with masked LM and NSP; here, the model predicts the masked word at position five.","左边，我们用掩码语言模型和 NSP 在无标注文本上预训练；这里模型在预测第 5 个位置被遮住的词。"],[479.63,485.83,115,"On the right, we take the same model and fine-tune it on a downstream task, here spam classification.","右边，我们拿同一个模型在下游任务上微调，这里是垃圾邮件分类。"],[486.18,494.3,115,"The key idea: all the weights are fine-tuned on the downstream task, and we only add a small task-specific output layer.","核心思想：所有权重都在下游任务上微调，我们只额外加一个小的、任务专用的输出层。"],[495.55,496.81,116,"A few more details.","再讲几个细节。"],[497.16,500.46,116,"The input representation is the sum of three embeddings.","输入表示是三种嵌入之和。"],[500.81,507.61,116,"Token embeddings; segment embeddings, to tell sentence A from sentence B; and position embeddings.","词元嵌入；用来区分句子 A 和句子 B 的段嵌入；以及位置嵌入。"],[507.96,516.27,116,"BERT uses word pieces instead of words. For example, playing becomes play, plus a continuation piece, ing.","BERT 用 WordPiece 子词而不是整词。比如 playing 被切成 play 和后续片段 ##ing。"],[516.62,518.57,116,"You'll work with this in Assignment four.","你们会在作业 4 中用到它。"],[518.92,528.25,116,"BERT was trained for about forty epochs on Wikipedia, two point five billion tokens, plus BookCorpus, zero point eight billion tokens.","BERT 在维基百科（25 亿词元）加 BookCorpus（8 亿词元）上训练了约 40 个 epoch。"],[528.6,533.3,116,"And it was released in two sizes: BERT base and BERT large.","它发布了两个规模：BERT base 和 BERT large。"],[534.55,541.09,117,"Let's walk through BERT from the data, to training, to inference, with one concrete example.","我们用一个具体例子，把 BERT 从数据、训练到推理完整走一遍。"],[541.44,546.35,117,"Step one is building a training example. BERT is trained on pairs of sentences.","第一步是构建训练样本。BERT 是在句子对上训练的。"],[546.7,551.66,117,"Here, sentence A is the man went to the store, and sentence B is he bought milk.","这里，句子 A 是 the man went to the store，句子 B 是 he bought milk。"],[552.01,560.61,117,"First, we add special tokens. CLS goes at the very beginning; its final vector will summarize the whole input.","首先，加入特殊标记。[CLS] 放在最开头，它最终的向量会概括整个输入。"],[560.96,564.11,117,"SEP marks the end of each sentence.","[SEP] 标记每个句子的结尾。"],[564.46,569.28,117,"Every token also gets a segment label, A or B, and a position index.","每个词元还会得到一个段标签 A 或 B，以及一个位置编号。"],[569.63,574.26,117,"Because B really follows A here, the NSP label is IsNext.","因为这里 B 确实紧跟在 A 之后，所以 NSP 标签是 IsNext。"],[574.61,581.4,117,"In half of the training pairs, B is replaced by a random sentence, and the label becomes NotNext.","在一半的训练样本中，B 会被换成一个随机句子，标签就变成 NotNext。"],[581.75,586.33,117,"Next, we pick about fifteen percent of the tokens as prediction targets.","接着，选出大约 15% 的词元作为预测目标。"],[586.68,591.0,117,"Here we select three, so that we can see all three cases.","这里我们选了三个，好让三种情况都能看到。"],[591.35,596.88,117,"Then we corrupt them. Eighty percent of the time, the token becomes MASK, like store.","然后对它们做破坏。80% 的情况下，词元变成 [MASK]，比如 store。"],[597.23,602.32,117,"Ten percent of the time, it becomes a random word, like milk turning into apple.","10% 的情况下变成一个随机词，比如 milk 变成了 apple。"],[602.67,607.22,117,"And ten percent of the time, it stays the same, like went.","还有 10% 的情况保持不变，比如 went。"],[607.57,616.2,117,"Finally, the targets: at the three selected positions, the model must recover the original words, went, store and milk.","最后是预测目标：在这三个选中的位置上，模型必须还原出原词 went、store 和 milk。"],[616.55,621.03,117,"And at CLS, it must predict IsNext.","在 [CLS] 位置上，它要预测 IsNext。"],[621.38,622.88,117,"Why not always use MASK?","为什么不总是用 [MASK]？"],[623.23,630.9,117,"Because MASK never appears when we use BERT later, so we want the model to build good representations for real words too.","因为之后使用 BERT 时 [MASK] 从不出现，所以我们希望模型也能为真实的词建立良好的表示。"],[632.15,634.87,118,"Step two: pre-training on this example.","第二步：用这个样本进行预训练。"],[635.22,644.82,118,"Each input token is turned into a vector: its token embedding, plus a segment embedding for A or B, plus a position embedding.","每个输入词元被转换成一个向量：词元嵌入，加上表示 A 或 B 的段嵌入，再加上位置嵌入。"],[645.17,655.34,118,"Now the first Transformer layer. All positions are updated in parallel, and the grey lines show that every position attends to every other position.","接下来是第一层 Transformer。所有位置并行更新，灰色的线表示每个位置都关注所有其他位置。"],[655.69,664.94,118,"For example, to guess the word at MASK, the model can use both the left context, the man went to the, and the right context, he bought apple.","例如，为了猜出 [MASK] 处的词，模型既能用左侧上下文 the man went to the，也能用右侧上下文 he bought apple。"],[665.29,670.26,118,"This is the key difference from GPT: BERT's encoder is bidirectional.","这就是和 GPT 的关键区别：BERT 的编码器是双向的。"],[670.61,675.51,118,"The second layer does the same again. Real BERT has twelve or twenty-four such layers.","第二层再做一次同样的事。真实的 BERT 有 12 层或 24 层。"],[675.86,680.26,118,"On top, we only make predictions at the selected positions.","在顶层，我们只在选中的位置上做预测。"],[680.61,688.59,118,"For the MASK position, the model gives store a probability of zero point six two, so the loss is minus log zero point six two.","在 [MASK] 位置，模型给 store 的概率是 0.62，所以损失是负的 log 0.62。"],[688.94,693.44,118,"At position ten, it must recover milk, even though it saw apple.","在第 10 个位置，即使它看到的是 apple，也必须还原出 milk。"],[693.79,700.83,118,"And at CLS, the NSP head predicts IsNext. These numbers are just for illustration.","在 [CLS] 位置，NSP 头预测 IsNext。这些数字只是示意。"],[701.18,708.28,118,"The total loss is the masked LM loss on the selected positions, plus the NSP loss.","总损失等于选中位置上的掩码语言模型损失，加上 NSP 损失。"],[708.63,715.87,118,"We backpropagate and update all the weights, and repeat this over billions of words from Wikipedia and BookCorpus.","我们反向传播、更新所有权重，并在维基百科和 BookCorpus 的数十亿词上反复进行。"],[717.12,720.47,119,"Step three: how do we use BERT after pre-training?","第三步：预训练之后怎么使用 BERT？"],[720.82,724.99,119,"The most common way is fine-tuning. Say we want sentiment classification.","最常见的方式是微调。比如我们想做情感分类。"],[725.34,729.64,119,"We feed CLS, the movie was great, SEP, into the pre-trained encoder.","我们把 [CLS] the movie was great [SEP] 输入预训练好的编码器。"],[729.99,738.27,119,"In one forward pass, every token looks at every other token in both layers, and all positions are computed at the same time.","在一次前向传播中，每个词元在两层里都看到所有其他词元，所有位置同时计算。"],[738.62,740.71,119,"There is no left-to-right loop.","没有从左到右的循环。"],[741.06,746.61,119,"We take only the final vector of CLS, and put a small new linear layer with softmax on top.","我们只取 [CLS] 的最终向量，在上面接一个新的小线性层加 softmax。"],[746.96,751.29,119,"It outputs positive with zero point nine four.","它输出“正面”，概率 0.94。"],[751.64,756.86,119,"During fine-tuning, we train this new layer together with all the BERT weights on labeled data.","微调时，我们在标注数据上把这个新层和 BERT 的全部权重一起训练。"],[757.21,763.61,119,"We can also use BERT without fine-tuning, with the masked LM head from pre-training.","也可以不微调，直接用预训练时的掩码语言模型头来使用 BERT。"],[763.96,769.69,119,"For Paris is the MASK of France, it predicts capital, using context on both sides.","对于 Paris is the [MASK] of France，它利用两侧的上下文预测出 capital。"],[770.04,780.47,119,"So, compared with GPT: BERT reads the whole input in one pass, and is great for understanding tasks like classification, question answering and embeddings.","所以和 GPT 相比：BERT 一次读完整个输入，非常适合分类、问答、向量表示等理解类任务。"],[780.82,787.5,119,"GPT runs a loop, adding one token per pass and looking only to the left, which makes it great for generation.","GPT 则是循环运行，每次只加一个词元、只看左边，因此非常适合生成。"],[787.85,795.32,119,"That is why the field split into these two families, and later, as we will see, why decoder-only models took over.","这就是为什么领域分成了这两大家族，也是后来解码器模型胜出的原因，我们后面会看到。"],[796.57,801.97,120,"This leads to a new paradigm: one pre-trained model is adapted everywhere.","这带来了一种新范式：一个预训练模型被适配到各种任务。"],[802.32,807.79,120,"The same BERT is fine-tuned for classification, question answering, tagging, and so on.","同一个 BERT 被微调用于分类、问答、序列标注等等。"],[808.14,811.58,120,"This is perhaps one of the first popular foundation models.","这或许是最早流行起来的基础模型之一。"],[811.93,819.03,120,"The term was introduced by Stanford in the paper, On the Opportunities and Risks of Foundation Models.","这个术语是斯坦福在论文《On the Opportunities and Risks of Foundation Models》中提出的。"],[820.28,823.09,121,"Here's how BERT is applied to different tasks.","这是 BERT 应用于不同任务的方式。"],[823.44,827.24,121,"Sentence-pair classification, like natural language inference.","句对分类，比如自然语言推理。"],[827.59,830.48,121,"Single-sentence classification, like sentiment.","单句分类，比如情感分析。"],[830.83,835.27,121,"Question answering, where we predict the start and end of the answer span.","问答，我们预测答案片段的起点和终点。"],[835.62,839.61,121,"And single-sentence tagging, like named entity recognition.","还有单句标注，比如命名实体识别。"],[839.96,845.28,121,"In each case, we use the same pre-trained model and just change the output layer.","每种情况都用同一个预训练模型，只更换输出层。"],[846.53,851.86,122,"What about scalability? This figure shows the effect of model size on BERT.","那可扩展性呢？这张图展示了模型规模对 BERT 的影响。"],[852.21,856.17,122,"As the model gets bigger, performance improves, but the curve flattens.","模型越大，性能越好，但曲线逐渐变平。"],[856.52,859.81,122,"It seems BERT cannot benefit that much from scaling.","看起来 BERT 并不能从扩大规模中获益太多。"],[861.06,862.88,123,"Now compare GPT.","再来对比 GPT。"],[863.23,870.16,123,"These plots show many tasks: modular arithmetic, transliteration, word unscrambling, and so on.","这些图展示了许多任务：模运算、音标转写、单词还原等等。"],[870.51,877.15,123,"As GPT models scale up, at some point performance suddenly jumps. People call this emergent abilities.","随着 GPT 类模型规模增大，性能会在某个点突然跃升。人们把这称为涌现能力。"],[877.5,879.51,123,"So GPT scales better.","所以 GPT 的扩展性更好。"],[879.86,885.6,123,"One explanation is that generation scales, while discrimination does not scale as well.","一种解释是：生成式任务能随规模扩展，而判别式任务没那么好。"],[885.95,891.9,123,"This is one of the main reasons the field moved from BERT-style models to GPT-style models.","这是领域从 BERT 式模型转向 GPT 式模型的主要原因之一。"]]},{"n":8,"title":"Encoders, Encoder-Decoders and Their Trade-offs","blurb":"RoBERTa, ALBERT and ELECTRA as encoder variants, T5 and BART as encoder-decoders, the three-way architecture trade-offs, and DeepSeek's causal encoder-decoder.","file":"lecture03_part8.mp4","poster":"lecture03_part8.jpg","duration":975.1,"first":124,"last":144,"slides":[{"n":124,"title":"Variants of Contextualized Word Vectors","t":0.8},{"n":125,"title":"Overview","t":6.22},{"n":126,"title":"Pretraining for Three Types of Architectures","t":38.27},{"n":127,"title":"RoBERTa","t":70.27},{"n":128,"title":"SpanBERT","t":100.48},{"n":129,"title":"BERT-wwm","t":127.0},{"n":130,"title":"ERNIE","t":149.5},{"n":131,"title":"ALBERT","t":177.02},{"n":132,"title":"ALBERT","t":210.6},{"n":133,"title":"ELECTRA","t":240.84},{"n":134,"title":"ELECTRA: Replaced Token Detection, Step by Step","t":277.37},{"n":135,"title":"Pretraining for Three Types of Architectures","t":352.77},{"n":136,"title":"Pretraining Encoder-decoders: What Pretraining Objective to Use?","t":364.16},{"n":137,"title":"Pretraining Encoder-decoders: What Pretraining Objective to Use?","t":386.27},{"n":138,"title":"Pretraining Encoder-decoders: What Pretraining Objective to Use?","t":418.88},{"n":139,"title":"T5: Many Tasks, One Text-to-Text Model","t":458.16},{"n":140,"title":"Pretraining Encoder-decoders: What Pretraining Objective to Use?","t":552.95},{"n":141,"title":"BART: Corrupt the Text, Then Reconstruct It","t":583.4},{"n":142,"title":"Pretraining for Three Types of Architectures","t":679.12},{"n":143,"title":"Encoder vs. Decoder vs. Encoder-Decoder: Trade-offs","t":694.15},{"n":144,"title":"Is Encoder-Decoder Back? DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","t":833.21}],"sents":[[0.8,4.82,124,"Next, let's look at some variants of contextualized word vectors.","接下来，我们看看上下文词向量的一些变体。"],[6.07,15.59,125,"Here's an overview table from our survey on pre-trained language models in the biomedical domain, published in ACM Computing Surveys.","这是我们发表在 ACM Computing Surveys 上的生物医学预训练语言模型综述里的一张概览表。"],[15.94,20.5,125,"It lists static models like Skip-Gram, CBOW and GloVe.","它列出了静态模型，比如 Skip-Gram、CBOW 和 GloVe。"],[20.85,28.98,125,"And contextualized ones like ELMo, BERT, RoBERTa, ALBERT, ELECTRA, T5, BART and GPT.","还有上下文相关的模型，比如 ELMo、BERT、RoBERTa、ALBERT、ELECTRA、T5、BART 和 GPT。"],[29.33,33.13,125,"For each, it shows the architecture and the training task.","每个模型都标出了架构和训练任务。"],[33.48,36.87,125,"You can use it as a map for the rest of this section.","你可以把它当作本节剩余内容的地图。"],[38.12,45.34,126,"Remember the three types of architectures. The architecture influences the type of pre-training, and the natural use cases.","还记得三类架构吗？架构决定了预训练的方式，也决定了天然的使用场景。"],[45.69,50.61,126,"Encoders get bidirectional context, so they can condition on the future.","编码器能看到双向上下文，所以可以以“未来”为条件。"],[50.96,54.45,126,"The question is how to train them to build strong representations.","问题是如何训练它们，才能得到强大的表示。"],[54.8,58.49,126,"Encoder-decoders try to combine the good parts of both.","编码器-解码器试图结合两者的优点。"],[58.84,66.73,126,"And decoders are the language models we have seen so far: nice to generate from, but they can't condition on future words.","解码器就是我们一直在讲的语言模型：很适合生成，但不能以后面的词为条件。"],[67.08,68.87,126,"We'll start with encoders.","我们先从编码器讲起。"],[70.12,78.98,127,"First, RoBERTa: A Robustly Optimized BERT Pretraining Approach, from the University of Washington and Facebook, two thousand nineteen.","首先是 RoBERTa：《A Robustly Optimized BERT Pretraining Approach》，来自华盛顿大学和 Facebook，2019 年。"],[79.33,82.35,127,"The finding is simple: BERT was under-trained.","结论很简单：BERT 训练得不够充分。"],[82.7,88.41,127,"Training for more epochs helps, even on the same data. More data also helps.","即使在相同数据上，多训练几个 epoch 也有帮助。更多数据同样有帮助。"],[88.76,92.6,127,"They also removed NSP and slightly improved the masking.","他们还去掉了 NSP，并稍微改进了掩码方式。"],[92.95,99.08,127,"As you can see in the table, RoBERTa beats BERT large and XLNet on most GLUE tasks.","从表中可以看到，RoBERTa 在大多数 GLUE 任务上超过了 BERT large 和 XLNet。"],[100.33,104.88,128,"SpanBERT, from Joshi and colleagues, two thousand nineteen.","SpanBERT，来自 Joshi 等人，2019 年。"],[105.23,110.9,128,"Instead of masking random individual tokens, it masks whole contiguous spans of text.","它不再随机遮住单个词元，而是遮住整段连续的文本。"],[111.25,116.0,128,"Here, the span, an American football game, is masked as a whole.","这里，an American football game 这一整段被一起遮住。"],[116.35,125.6,128,"This makes the task harder and more meaningful, and it helps a lot on span-based tasks like question answering, as the SQuAD results show.","这让任务更难也更有意义，对问答这类基于片段的任务帮助很大，正如 SQuAD 的结果所示。"],[126.85,133.18,129,"BERT-wwm: whole word masking for Chinese, by Cui and colleagues, two thousand nineteen.","BERT-wwm：面向中文的全词掩码，来自崔一鸣等人，2019 年。"],[133.53,136.36,129,"In Chinese BERT, tokens are characters.","在中文 BERT 中，词元是单个汉字。"],[136.71,141.96,129,"If we mask only one character of a word, the model can easily guess it from the other characters.","如果只遮住一个词中的一个字，模型很容易根据其他字猜出来。"],[142.31,148.1,129,"So BERT-wwm masks all the characters of a whole Chinese word at once.","所以 BERT-wwm 会把一个中文词的所有字一起遮住。"],[149.35,157.73,130,"ERNIE: Enhanced Language Representation with Informative Entities, by Zhang and colleagues, two thousand nineteen.","ERNIE：《Enhanced Language Representation with Informative Entities》，来自张正彦等人，2019 年。"],[158.08,163.96,130,"The idea is to mask informative entities, and to bring in knowledge from a knowledge graph.","思路是遮住信息量大的实体，并引入知识图谱中的知识。"],[164.31,172.68,130,"For example, in Bob Dylan wrote Blowin' in the Wind, the entities Bob Dylan and the song title are linked to the knowledge graph.","比如在 Bob Dylan wrote Blowin' in the Wind 中，实体 Bob Dylan 和歌名都被链接到知识图谱上。"],[173.03,175.62,130,"So the model learns factual knowledge.","这样模型就学到了事实性知识。"],[176.87,183.14,131,"ALBERT: A Lite BERT, from Google and TTI Chicago, two thousand nineteen. It has two innovations.","ALBERT：轻量版 BERT，来自谷歌和芝加哥丰田技术研究院，2019 年。它有两个创新。"],[183.49,187.01,131,"The first one is factorized embedding parameterization.","第一个是嵌入参数的因式分解。"],[187.36,195.53,131,"Instead of a large embedding matrix of one hundred thousand times one thousand twenty-four, we use a small embedding size, say one hundred twenty-eight.","不用 10 万乘 1024 的大嵌入矩阵，而是用较小的嵌入维度，比如 128。"],[195.88,199.98,131,"Then we project it to the hidden size of one thousand twenty-four.","然后再投影到 1024 维的隐藏层大小。"],[200.33,209.2,131,"So that's one hundred thousand times one hundred twenty-eight, plus one hundred twenty-eight times one thousand twenty-four, which is far fewer parameters.","也就是 10 万乘 128，加上 128 乘 1024，参数量少得多。"],[210.45,216.53,132,"The second innovation is cross-layer parameter sharing: all Transformer layers share the same parameters.","第二个创新是跨层参数共享：所有 Transformer 层共用同一套参数。"],[216.88,222.07,132,"The results are comparable to BERT, with far fewer parameters.","结果与 BERT 相当，参数却少得多。"],[222.42,230.34,132,"In the lower table, ALBERT large has only eighteen million parameters, compared with three hundred thirty-four million for BERT large.","下面的表中，ALBERT large 只有 1800 万参数，而 BERT large 有 3.34 亿。"],[230.69,235.57,132,"But note: ALBERT is light in terms of parameters, not in terms of speed.","但要注意：ALBERT 轻的是参数量，而不是速度。"],[235.92,239.44,132,"Since we still run every layer, the computation is about the same.","因为每一层仍然都要计算，计算量基本没变。"],[240.69,250.17,133,"ELECTRA: Pre-training Text Encoders as Discriminators Rather Than Generators, by Clark and colleagues, two thousand twenty.","ELECTRA：《Pre-training Text Encoders as Discriminators Rather Than Generators》，来自 Clark 等人，2020 年。"],[250.52,257.44,133,"A small generator, which is a small masked LM, replaces some tokens with plausible alternatives.","一个小生成器，也就是一个小型掩码语言模型，把一些词元替换成看似合理的候选词。"],[257.79,260.24,133,"Here, cooked becomes ate.","这里，cooked 变成了 ate。"],[260.59,267.88,133,"Then the main model, the discriminator, must decide for every token whether it is original or replaced.","然后主模型，也就是判别器，要对每个词元判断它是原词还是被替换过的。"],[268.23,275.97,133,"The advantage is that the model learns from all tokens, not just fifteen percent, so it is much more compute-efficient.","好处是模型从所有词元中学习，而不只是 15%，因此计算效率高得多。"],[277.22,283.15,134,"Let's see ELECTRA's training step by step, with the example from the paper: the chef cooked the meal.","我们用论文里的例子 the chef cooked the meal，一步步看 ELECTRA 的训练。"],[283.5,290.89,134,"First, as in BERT, we mask about fifteen percent of the tokens. Here, the and cooked become MASK.","首先，和 BERT 一样，遮住约 15% 的词元。这里 the 和 cooked 变成了 [MASK]。"],[291.24,298.31,134,"Then a small generator, which is just a small masked language model, fills in each blank by sampling a word.","然后一个小生成器，也就是一个小的掩码语言模型，为每个空采样一个词来填上。"],[298.66,303.88,134,"It gets the first one right, the, but for the second one it samples ate.","第一个它填对了，是 the；第二个它采样出了 ate。"],[304.23,309.49,134,"The chef ate the meal is a perfectly plausible sentence, which makes it a hard fake.","The chef ate the meal 是一个完全合理的句子，所以这是一个很难识破的假词。"],[309.84,316.2,134,"Now the main model, the discriminator, which is ELECTRA itself, reads the corrupted sentence.","现在主模型，也就是判别器，即 ELECTRA 本身，读取这个被破坏的句子。"],[316.55,321.8,134,"It makes a decision for every single token: is it original, or was it replaced?","它对每一个词元都做出判断：是原词，还是被替换过？"],[322.15,324.76,134,"Here, only ate is replaced.","这里只有 ate 是被替换的。"],[325.11,330.68,134,"Note that the first the counts as original, because the generator happened to produce the true word.","注意，第一个 the 算作原词，因为生成器恰好生成了真实的词。"],[331.03,336.96,134,"Why does this help? In BERT, only the fifteen percent masked tokens give a training signal.","为什么这样更好？在 BERT 中，只有那 15% 被遮住的词元提供训练信号。"],[337.31,345.46,134,"In ELECTRA, the loss covers all tokens, so we learn much more from each sentence, and ELECTRA matches BERT with far less compute.","在 ELECTRA 中，损失覆盖所有词元，每句话能学到更多，ELECTRA 用少得多的算力就能达到 BERT 的水平。"],[345.81,351.37,134,"After pre-training, we throw the generator away and fine-tune only the discriminator.","预训练结束后，生成器被丢弃，只微调判别器。"],[352.62,360.37,135,"Now let's move to the second type: encoder-decoders, which try to combine the good parts of encoders and decoders.","现在进入第二类：编码器-解码器，它试图结合编码器和解码器的优点。"],[360.72,362.76,135,"What's the best way to pre-train them?","预训练它们的最佳方式是什么？"],[364.01,373.02,136,"For encoder-decoders, we could do something like language modeling, but where a prefix of every input is given to the encoder, and is not predicted.","对于编码器-解码器，可以做类似语言建模的事，但每个输入的前缀交给编码器，且不被预测。"],[373.37,378.23,136,"The encoder benefits from bidirectional context over the prefix.","编码器可以在前缀上利用双向上下文。"],[378.58,384.87,136,"And the decoder is trained with language modeling to generate the rest, and this trains the whole model.","解码器则用语言建模来生成剩余部分，以此训练整个模型。"],[386.12,392.02,137,"What Raffel and colleagues found to work best was span corruption. Their model is T5.","Raffel 等人发现效果最好的是片段破坏。他们的模型就是 T5。"],[392.37,398.3,137,"We replace spans of different lengths in the input with unique placeholders, like X and Y.","我们把输入中长度不一的片段替换成唯一的占位符，比如 X 和 Y。"],[398.65,401.83,137,"Then the decoder has to output the missing spans.","然后解码器要输出被删掉的片段。"],[402.18,409.66,137,"For example: thank you X me to your party Y week; and the target is for inviting, and last.","例如：Thank you <X> me to your party <Y> week，目标输出是 for inviting 和 last。"],[410.01,417.48,137,"This is implemented in text preprocessing, so on the decoder side it still looks like language modeling.","这是在文本预处理中实现的，所以在解码器一侧看起来仍然像语言建模。"],[418.73,428.65,138,"A fascinating property of T5: it can be fine-tuned to answer a wide range of questions, retrieving knowledge from its own parameters, without looking anything up.","T5 有个很有意思的特性：微调后它能回答各种各样的问题，从自身参数中提取知识，而无需查找任何资料。"],[429.0,435.7,138,"For example, asked when Franklin D. Roosevelt was born, it answers eighteen eighty-two.","比如问富兰克林·罗斯福哪年出生，它回答 1882。"],[436.05,442.81,138,"The table shows results on open-domain versions of Natural Questions, WebQuestions and TriviaQA.","表格展示了在开放域版本的 Natural Questions、WebQuestions 和 TriviaQA 上的结果。"],[443.16,451.83,138,"As the model grows from two hundred twenty million to eleven billion parameters, performance keeps improving, and gets close to retrieval-based systems.","随着模型从 2.2 亿参数增长到 110 亿参数，性能持续提升，逐渐接近基于检索的系统。"],[452.18,456.76,138,"This shows that language models can act as a kind of knowledge base.","这说明语言模型可以充当某种知识库。"],[458.01,466.3,139,"T5's big idea is to treat every task as text-to-text: the input is text, and the output is also text.","T5 的核心思想是把所有任务都当作文本到文本：输入是文本，输出也是文本。"],[466.65,472.6,139,"Let's start with pre-training. The original sentence is: thank you for inviting me to your party last week.","先看预训练。原句是：Thank you for inviting me to your party last week。"],[472.95,480.08,139,"We drop two spans, for inviting and last, and replace each with a sentinel token, X and Y.","我们删掉两个片段 for inviting 和 last，分别替换成哨兵标记 X 和 Y。"],[480.43,484.23,139,"Note that there is no task prefix during pre-training.","注意，预训练时没有任务前缀。"],[484.58,494.65,139,"The target is just the dropped spans, each introduced by its sentinel: X, for inviting, Y, last; and a final sentinel Z marks the end.","目标输出就是被删掉的片段，各自由哨兵引出：X for inviting，Y last；最后一个哨兵 Z 表示结束。"],[495.0,498.36,139,"So even pre-training is text in, text out.","所以连预训练也是文本进、文本出。"],[498.71,507.88,139,"Now fine-tuning. For translation, the input starts with the prefix, translate English to German, and the model writes Das ist gut.","再看微调。对于翻译，输入以前缀 translate English to German 开头，模型输出 Das ist gut。"],[508.23,516.37,139,"For CoLA, a grammaticality task, the prefix is cola sentence, and the model literally writes the words not acceptable.","对于语法可接受性任务 CoLA，前缀是 cola sentence，模型直接写出 not acceptable 这几个词。"],[516.72,525.76,139,"STS-B is a regression task about sentence similarity, but T5 simply writes the number three point eight as text.","STS-B 是关于句子相似度的回归任务，但 T5 只是把数字 3.8 当作文本写出来。"],[526.11,530.5,139,"And for summarization, the prefix summarize gives a short summary.","对于摘要任务，前缀 summarize 让它生成一段简短的摘要。"],[530.85,541.17,139,"All of these tasks are mixed into one training set and trained together: same model, same loss, which is to predict the output text, and same decoding.","所有这些任务被混合成一个训练集一起训练：同一个模型、同一个损失，也就是预测输出文本，以及同样的解码方式。"],[541.52,544.52,139,"Only the prefix tells the model which task to do.","只有前缀告诉模型要做哪个任务。"],[544.87,551.55,139,"This text in, text out idea is exactly how we use today's large language models with prompts.","这种文本进、文本出的思想，正是我们今天用提示词使用大语言模型的方式。"],[552.8,560.07,140,"Another important encoder-decoder model is BART, from Facebook: Denoising Sequence-to-Sequence Pre-training.","另一个重要的编码器-解码器模型是 Facebook 的 BART：去噪的序列到序列预训练。"],[560.42,570.52,140,"BART corrupts the input in several ways, such as masking, deleting and shuffling, and trains the model to reconstruct the original text.","BART 用多种方式破坏输入，比如掩码、删除和打乱，再训练模型重建原文。"],[570.87,577.41,140,"You can see it as a combination of BERT's bidirectional encoder and GPT's autoregressive decoder.","可以把它看作 BERT 的双向编码器与 GPT 的自回归解码器的结合。"],[577.76,582.0,140,"BART works especially well for generation tasks like summarization.","BART 在摘要等生成任务上表现尤其好。"],[583.25,592.16,141,"BART is an encoder-decoder model trained as a denoising autoencoder: we damage a text, and the model must repair it.","BART 是一个按去噪自编码器方式训练的编码器-解码器模型：我们破坏文本，模型负责修复。"],[592.51,597.31,141,"Our document is: the cat sat on the mat. It was very happy.","我们的文档是：The cat sat on the mat. It was very happy."],[597.66,602.76,141,"Here are five ways to damage it. Token masking replaces single words with MASK.","这里有五种破坏方式。词元掩码把单个词替换成 [MASK]。"],[603.11,609.34,141,"Token deletion removes words, so the model must also find where they were.","词元删除直接删掉词，所以模型还得找出它们原来在哪儿。"],[609.69,614.27,141,"Text infilling replaces a whole span with one MASK.","文本填充用一个 [MASK] 替换一整段文本。"],[614.62,622.42,141,"Sentence permutation shuffles the sentences, and document rotation starts the text at a random word.","句子重排会打乱句子顺序，文档旋转则让文本从一个随机的词开始。"],[622.77,630.3,141,"Let's take text infilling. The first MASK hides three words, sat on the, and the second MASK hides nothing at all.","以文本填充为例。第一个 [MASK] 藏了三个词 sat on the，第二个 [MASK] 什么也没藏。"],[630.65,635.8,141,"So the model cannot just fill in one word per mask; it has to figure out how many words are missing.","所以模型不能每个掩码只填一个词，它必须判断缺了多少个词。"],[636.15,643.5,141,"The damaged text goes into the encoder, which reads the whole input at once: every word can look at the words on both sides.","被破坏的文本进入编码器，编码器一次读完整个输入：每个词都能看到两侧的词。"],[643.85,648.39,141,"Then the decoder writes the original text, one word at a time.","然后解码器一次一个词地写出原文。"],[648.74,657.91,141,"At each step, it looks at the encoder's output and at the words it has already written. Here, it restores sat on the.","每一步它都参考编码器的输出和已经写出的词。这里，它还原出了 sat on the。"],[658.26,663.07,141,"The training signal compares every word the decoder writes with the original text.","训练信号就是把解码器写出的每个词与原文进行比较。"],[663.42,668.45,141,"Because the output is always the complete original, we can use any kind of damage.","因为输出总是完整的原文，所以可以使用任何一种破坏方式。"],[668.8,677.72,141,"In the paper, text infilling together with sentence permutation worked best, and BART became very strong for summarization and translation.","论文中，文本填充加句子重排的效果最好，BART 在摘要和翻译上都非常强。"],[678.97,681.78,142,"Finally, the third type: decoders.","最后是第三类：解码器。"],[682.13,688.69,142,"These are the language models we have seen so far: nice to generate from, but they can't condition on future words.","这就是我们一直在讲的语言模型：很适合生成，但不能以后面的词为条件。"],[689.04,692.75,142,"And this is the type that eventually took over the field.","而正是这一类最终统治了整个领域。"],[694.0,698.16,143,"Before we move on to GPT, let me put the three architecture families side by side.","在讲 GPT 之前，我们把三大架构家族放在一起比较一下。"],[698.51,704.75,143,"Encoder-only models like BERT use bidirectional attention: every token sees the whole input.","像 BERT 这样的纯编码器模型使用双向注意力：每个词元都能看到整个输入。"],[705.1,712.36,143,"They are trained with masked language modeling, so they learn from only about fifteen percent of the tokens.","它们用掩码语言建模训练，所以只从大约 15% 的词元中学习。"],[712.71,720.86,143,"They are excellent for understanding tasks such as classification, retrieval and embeddings, but they cannot naturally generate text.","它们非常擅长分类、检索、向量表示等理解类任务，但不能自然地生成文本。"],[721.21,729.36,143,"Decoder-only models like GPT and LLaMA use causal attention: each token sees only the tokens before it.","像 GPT 和 LLaMA 这样的纯解码器模型使用因果注意力：每个词元只能看到它之前的词元。"],[729.71,734.66,143,"They are trained with next-token prediction, so every single token is a training signal.","它们用下一个词元预测来训练，所以每个词元都是训练信号。"],[735.01,743.81,143,"Encoder-decoder models like T5 and BART combine a bidirectional encoder with a causal decoder that cross-attends to it.","像 T5 和 BART 这样的编码器-解码器模型，把双向编码器和一个对它做交叉注意力的因果解码器结合起来。"],[744.16,749.1,143,"They are very good at input-to-output tasks such as translation and summarization.","它们非常擅长翻译、摘要这类输入到输出的任务。"],[749.45,753.45,143,"Now look at the KV cache row, because it matters a lot in practice.","现在看 KV 缓存这一行，它在实际中非常重要。"],[753.8,761.74,143,"When a decoder-only model generates text, the keys and values of past tokens never change, since no token can look at the future.","纯解码器模型生成文本时，过去词元的键和值永远不会改变，因为没有词元能看到未来。"],[762.09,767.0,143,"So we compute them once, store them, and reuse them for every new token.","所以我们只算一次、存下来，每生成一个新词元就重复使用。"],[767.35,775.93,143,"The prompt and the output share one cache, and we can even reuse it across turns of a conversation. That is called prefix caching.","提示和输出共享同一个缓存，甚至能在多轮对话之间复用。这叫做前缀缓存。"],[776.28,784.01,143,"An encoder-only model has no such incremental cache: if the input changes, it has to re-encode everything.","纯编码器模型没有这种增量缓存：输入一变，就得全部重新编码。"],[784.36,787.81,143,"An encoder-decoder model can cache inside the decoder.","编码器-解码器模型可以在解码器内部做缓存。"],[788.16,795.55,143,"But because its encoder is bidirectional, adding a new user turn changes the representation of all earlier tokens.","但由于它的编码器是双向的，加入新一轮用户输入会改变之前所有词元的表示。"],[795.9,799.19,143,"So in a chat setting, it has to re-encode the input.","所以在对话场景中，它必须重新编码输入。"],[799.54,803.76,143,"So why do decoder-only models dominate today? Four reasons.","那么，为什么今天纯解码器模型占据主导？有四个原因。"],[804.11,808.9,143,"One: a single simple objective on raw text, which scales easily.","第一：在原始文本上只有一个简单的目标，很容易扩展规模。"],[809.25,816.95,143,"Two: one interface for every task, text in and text out, which gives us prompting, in-context learning and chat.","第二：所有任务共用一个接口，文本进、文本出，由此有了提示、上下文学习和对话。"],[817.3,821.17,143,"Three: cheap generation, thanks to the KV cache.","第三：得益于 KV 缓存，生成成本很低。"],[821.52,831.81,143,"And four: predictable scaling, plus an entire ecosystem of tools built around this design, like FlashAttention, paged KV caches and speculative decoding.","第四：可预测的扩展规律，以及围绕这种设计构建的整套工具生态，比如 FlashAttention、分页 KV 缓存和投机解码。"],[833.06,840.29,144,"But is the story really over for encoder-decoder models? A very recent release makes this an interesting question.","但编码器-解码器模型的故事真的结束了吗？最近的一个发布让这个问题变得很有意思。"],[840.64,847.59,144,"On September tenth, two thousand twenty-six, DeepSeek released V4.1-Flash.","2026 年 9 月 10 日，DeepSeek 发布了 V4.1-Flash。"],[847.94,854.56,144,"It is a five hundred fifty-two billion parameter mixture-of-experts model, with a one-million-token context.","它是一个 5520 亿参数的混合专家模型，上下文长度一百万词元。"],[854.91,860.11,144,"DeepSeek describes its architecture as a Causal Encoder-Decoder.","DeepSeek 把它的架构称为“因果编码器-解码器”。"],[860.46,865.73,144,"The forty layers are split into a twenty-layer causal encoder and a twenty-layer decoder.","40 层被分成一个 20 层的因果编码器和一个 20 层的解码器。"],[866.08,873.66,144,"Look at the diagram. When the model reads the prompt, only the encoder runs, with about eight billion active parameters.","看这张图。模型读取提示时，只运行编码器，激活参数约 80 亿。"],[874.01,880.35,144,"When it writes the output, the full stack runs, with about sixteen billion active parameters.","写输出时，整个堆栈都运行，激活参数约 160 亿。"],[880.7,884.07,144,"The decoder does not build its own cache in every layer.","解码器并不在每一层都建立自己的缓存。"],[884.42,890.36,144,"Instead, it reads one shared global KV cache that is projected from the encoder's output.","而是读取一个由编码器输出投影得到的共享全局 KV 缓存。"],[890.71,899.12,144,"According to DeepSeek, this cuts the KV cache to about one quarter of the GPU memory of the previous V4-Flash model.","据 DeepSeek 介绍，这让 KV 缓存所需的显存降到上一代 V4-Flash 的约四分之一。"],[899.47,903.77,144,"So, has DeepSeek revived the encoder-decoder architecture?","那么，DeepSeek 是否复兴了编码器-解码器架构？"],[904.12,908.3,144,"My answer is: the name is back, but the T5 recipe is not.","我的回答是：名字回来了，但 T5 的那套做法没有回来。"],[908.65,914.09,144,"The key word is causal. Unlike T5, this encoder is not bidirectional.","关键词是“因果”。和 T5 不同，这个编码器不是双向的。"],[914.44,920.69,144,"Earlier tokens never depend on later ones, so all the cached states stay valid as the prompt grows.","前面的词元从不依赖后面的词元，所以随着提示变长，所有缓存的状态依然有效。"],[921.04,925.03,144,"That keeps the biggest advantage of decoder-only models that we just discussed.","这保住了我们刚才讨论的纯解码器模型的最大优势。"],[925.38,931.07,144,"In fact, the model is still a left-to-right language model, trained with next-token prediction.","事实上，它仍然是一个从左到右的语言模型，用下一个词元预测来训练。"],[931.42,940.2,144,"Conceptually, it is close to the decoder-decoder idea from the YOCO paper, You Only Cache Once, by Sun and colleagues in two thousand twenty-four.","从概念上说，它接近 Sun 等人 2024 年 YOCO 论文《You Only Cache Once》中的“解码器-解码器”思想。"],[940.55,949.07,144,"What really comes back is the encoder-decoder idea of splitting the work between reading the input and writing the output.","真正回归的，是编码器-解码器把“读输入”和“写输出”分工的思想。"],[949.42,954.54,144,"Here it is used for efficiency: a smaller KV cache and cheaper long prompts.","在这里它被用来提高效率：更小的 KV 缓存，更便宜的长提示。"],[954.89,960.9,144,"That matters a lot for coding assistants, which send very long contexts again and again.","这对编程助手非常重要，因为它们会一次又一次地发送很长的上下文。"],[961.25,966.4,144,"One caveat: DeepSeek's official release gives only a short description.","需要提醒的是：DeepSeek 的官方发布只给出了简短的描述。"],[966.75,973.22,144,"Some of the internal details come from third-party analyses, so please check the technical report when it comes out.","一些内部细节来自第三方分析，技术报告发布后请再核实。"]]},{"n":9,"title":"Back to Next-Word Prediction: GPT, LLaMA and Beyond","blurb":"Pretraining decoders, from GPT-2 generations and GPT-3 in-context learning to open LLaMA models, closing with why today's large models are decoder-only.","file":"lecture03_part9.mp4","poster":"lecture03_part9.jpg","duration":360.8,"first":145,"last":153,"slides":[{"n":145,"title":"Back to the Language Model (Next-word Prediction)","t":0.8},{"n":146,"title":"Pretraining Decoders","t":8.3},{"n":147,"title":"Pretraining Decoders","t":36.76},{"n":148,"title":"Increasingly Convincing Generations (GPT2) [Radford et al., 2019]","t":69.42},{"n":149,"title":"GPT-3, In-context Learning, and Very Large Models","t":106.36},{"n":150,"title":"LLaMA, Open-Source Models","t":142.43},{"n":151,"title":"Final questions to be answered","t":179.05},{"n":152,"title":"Acknowledgement","t":255.56},{"n":153,"title":"Claude Opus 5.5 is all you need!","t":275.42}],"sents":[[0.8,6.9,145,"So we are back to the language model in its original sense: next-word prediction.","于是我们又回到了最原本意义上的语言模型：预测下一个词。"],[8.15,13.89,146,"When using a pre-trained decoder for classification, we can ignore that it was trained as a language model.","用预训练解码器做分类时，可以不管它原本是作为语言模型训练的。"],[14.24,21.69,146,"We take the hidden state of the last word, and train a classifier on it: y equals A h T plus b.","我们取最后一个词的隐藏状态，在上面训练一个分类器：y 等于 A 乘 h_T 加 b。"],[22.04,29.69,146,"Here, A and b are randomly initialized and learned for the downstream task, and gradients flow through the whole network.","这里 A 和 b 是随机初始化、针对下游任务学习的，梯度会反向传播到整个网络。"],[30.04,35.36,146,"Note that this linear layer has not been pre-trained, so it must be learned from scratch.","注意，这个线性层没有经过预训练，必须从零开始学习。"],[36.61,39.91,147,"But it's more natural to use decoders as generators.","但更自然的做法是把解码器当作生成器使用。"],[40.26,46.02,147,"We pre-train them as language models, and then fine-tune the same next-word distribution.","先把它们作为语言模型预训练，再微调同一个下一个词的分布。"],[46.37,52.17,147,"This is helpful when the output is a sequence with a vocabulary like the one in pre-training.","当输出是一个序列，且词表与预训练时相似时，这很有用。"],[52.52,61.02,147,"For example, dialogue, where the context is the dialogue history, or summarization, where the context is the document.","比如对话，上下文是对话历史；或者摘要，上下文是文档。"],[61.37,68.02,147,"Here, A and b were pre-trained as part of the language model. Nothing is learned from scratch.","这里 A 和 b 是作为语言模型的一部分预训练过的。没有什么需要从零学起。"],[69.27,72.88,148,"And the generations became increasingly convincing.","生成的文本也越来越令人信服。"],[73.23,83.56,148,"GPT-2, a larger version of GPT with one point five billion parameters, trained on more data, produced surprisingly fluent text.","GPT-2 是 GPT 的更大版本，有 15 亿参数，用更多数据训练，生成的文本流畅得惊人。"],[83.91,92.94,148,"In this famous example, the human writes that scientists discovered a herd of unicorns in the Andes that spoke perfect English.","在这个著名的例子中，人类写道：科学家在安第斯山脉发现了一群会说流利英语的独角兽。"],[93.29,100.14,148,"And GPT-2 continues with a coherent story, about Ovid's Unicorn and Dr. Jorge Perez.","GPT-2 接着写出了一个连贯的故事，讲到了“奥维德独角兽”和豪尔赫·佩雷斯博士。"],[100.49,104.96,148,"This was in two thousand nineteen, and many people were shocked.","那是在 2019 年，很多人都震惊了。"],[106.21,110.91,149,"Then came GPT-3, in-context learning, and very large models.","接着是 GPT-3、上下文学习，以及超大规模模型。"],[111.26,119.98,149,"So far, we have used pre-trained models in two ways: sample from them, maybe with a prompt, or fine-tune them on a task.","到目前为止，我们用预训练模型的方式有两种：从中采样，可能加上提示；或者在某个任务上微调。"],[120.33,127.43,149,"But very large language models seem to learn without any gradient steps, just from examples you give in the context.","但超大语言模型似乎不需要任何梯度更新，仅凭你在上下文里给的例子就能学习。"],[127.78,129.8,149,"This is called in-context learning.","这叫做上下文学习。"],[130.15,141.03,149,"GPT-3 is the canonical example. For comparison, the largest T5 had eleven billion parameters, and GPT-3 has one hundred seventy-five billion.","GPT-3 是典型代表。作为对比，最大的 T5 有 110 亿参数，而 GPT-3 有 1750 亿。"],[142.28,150.68,150,"Finally, open-source models. LLaMA, from Meta, was a turning point for academic research on large language models.","最后是开源模型。Meta 的 LLaMA 是大语言模型学术研究的一个转折点。"],[151.03,156.52,150,"It can be customized for many use cases, especially in research and non-commercial projects.","它可以针对多种用途定制，尤其适合研究和非商业项目。"],[156.87,167.17,150,"And through architectural and training optimizations, it reached performance similar to GPT-3 with far fewer parameters and less compute.","通过架构和训练上的优化，它用少得多的参数和算力达到了与 GPT-3 相近的性能。"],[167.52,173.75,150,"Since then, the open model ecosystem has grown a lot, with models like Qwen, DeepSeek and Mistral.","此后，开源模型生态蓬勃发展，出现了 Qwen、DeepSeek、Mistral 等模型。"],[174.1,177.65,150,"Many of your course projects will build on open models like these.","你们的很多课程项目都会基于这样的开源模型。"],[178.9,182.39,151,"Before we wrap up, I'd like to leave you with two questions.","在结束之前，我想给大家留两个问题。"],[182.74,187.42,151,"We've already touched on both today, so try to answer them in your own words.","今天我们其实都已经谈到了，请试着用自己的话来回答。"],[187.77,191.3,151,"First, why is language modeling so powerful in AI?","第一，为什么语言建模在人工智能中如此强大？"],[191.65,199.56,151,"Think back to the beginning of the lecture. Next-word prediction needs no labels, so the data scales almost without limit.","回想一下这节课的开头。预测下一个词不需要标注，所以数据几乎可以无限扩展。"],[199.91,210.11,151,"And remember the fill-in-the-blank examples: to predict the next word well, a model has to pick up arithmetic, sentiment, coreference and commonsense knowledge.","还记得那些填空的例子吗：要把下一个词预测好，模型必须掌握算术、情感、指代和常识知识。"],[210.46,215.47,151,"Language is also how we store knowledge, how we reason, and how we talk to machines.","语言也是我们存储知识、进行推理、与机器交流的方式。"],[215.82,220.64,151,"So one simple objective ends up teaching an enormous amount.","所以，一个简单的目标最终能教会模型非常多的东西。"],[220.99,226.43,151,"Second, why are almost all of today's big models decoder-only, autoregressive models?","第二，为什么今天几乎所有大模型都是纯解码器的自回归模型？"],[226.78,234.36,151,"Go back to the trade-off table. Every token gives a training signal, and every task fits into text in and text out.","回到那张权衡对比表。每个词元都提供训练信号，每个任务都能纳入文本进、文本出的形式。"],[234.71,239.82,151,"Generation is cheap thanks to the KV cache, and scaling has been very predictable.","得益于 KV 缓存，生成成本很低，而且扩展规律非常可预测。"],[240.17,248.56,151,"BERT-style models are still great for understanding and embeddings, but for general-purpose models, the decoder has won, at least for now.","BERT 式模型在理解和向量表示上依然出色，但在通用模型上，解码器已经胜出，至少目前如此。"],[248.91,254.16,151,"If you can explain both answers to a friend, you've got the core of today's lecture.","如果你能把这两个答案讲给朋友听，你就掌握了今天这节课的核心。"],[255.41,269.44,152,"Before we finish, I want to acknowledge that many slides in this lecture are adapted from Princeton COS 484, Illinois CS447, and Stanford CS224n.","在结束之前，我要致谢：本讲的许多幻灯片改编自普林斯顿 COS 484、伊利诺伊 CS447 和斯坦福 CS224n。"],[269.79,274.02,152,"Thanks to those instructors for sharing their excellent materials.","感谢这些课程的老师分享了如此出色的材料。"],[275.27,277.64,153,"And one last slide, just for fun.","最后一张幻灯片，轻松一下。"],[277.99,287.4,153,"The title is a little joke on Attention is all you need, and on the ChatGPT is all you need sentence we scored at the very start of today.","这个标题是在调侃 Attention is all you need，也呼应了今天一开头我们打分的那句 ChatGPT is all you need。"],[287.75,294.59,153,"Here's what's behind it. Remember the spin-the-wheel animation, where we generated text from the Alice passage one word at a time?","它背后是这样的：还记得那个转轮盘的动画吗？我们用 Alice 那段文字一次生成一个词。"],[294.94,299.78,153,"This is the kind of request I gave Claude Opus 5.5 to build it.","这就是我让 Claude Opus 5.5 做这个动画时给出的请求。"],[300.13,304.83,153,"Our sampling slide with the roulette picture, and the Alice passage.","我们那张带轮盘图片的采样幻灯片，以及 Alice 那段文字。"],[305.18,312.36,153,"And a short instruction in Chinese, asking for a Russian-roulette animation that generates five words from this example.","还有一句简短的中文指令，要求用俄罗斯轮盘赌做一个用这个例子生成五个词的动画。"],[312.71,319.51,153,"And notice the irony: the model that helped me teach you n-gram sampling is itself doing exactly the same thing underneath.","注意其中的讽刺：帮我教你们 n-gram 采样的这个模型，底层做的正是完全相同的事。"],[319.86,326.7,153,"It predicts the next token again and again, just with a far better distribution than our bigram counts.","它一次又一次地预测下一个词元，只是它的分布比我们的二元组计数好得多。"],[327.05,335.31,153,"So, today's takeaways. A language model assigns probabilities to text, or equivalently, predicts the next word.","那么，今天的要点：语言模型给文本分配概率，等价地说，就是预测下一个词。"],[335.66,340.9,153,"We built it from counting, with n-grams, smoothing and perplexity.","我们从计数开始构建它，讲了 n-gram、平滑和困惑度。"],[341.25,347.91,153,"Then we moved to neural models, word vectors, ELMo, BERT, T5 and GPT.","然后进入神经模型、词向量、ELMo、BERT、T5 和 GPT。"],[348.26,356.2,153,"Next time, in Lecture four, we'll open the black box properly and study the Transformer, the architecture behind all of these models.","下次第四讲，我们将真正打开黑箱，学习 Transformer，也就是所有这些模型背后的架构。"],[356.55,358.99,153,"Thank you, and see you next time!","谢谢大家，下次见！"]]}]};
